var tafsirData_20 = {
  "1": "\"Ta-Ha\" opens Surah Taha with two disconnected letters, a feature found at the beginning of several chapters of the Quran. As with other such openings, their exact meaning is known best to God. The early scholars offered various interpretations, but the core point is that these letters are part of the revelation and a sign of the Quran's divine origin.\n\nSome scholars suggested that \"Ta-Ha\" is one of the names of the Prophet Muhammad. Others said it is an oath. Others said the letters carry meanings related to God's names and attributes. None of these interpretations is confirmed by a sound hadith, so they should be treated as possible explanations rather than certain facts.\n\nWhat is certain is that these letters draw the listener's attention to what follows. They signal that the words about to be recited are weighty and should be received with care. The same letters that make up ordinary Arabic speech are arranged by God into a book that no human being could produce.\n\nAfter these letters, the chapter will tell the story of Moses in great detail. It will describe his call at the burning bush, his encounter with Pharaoh, the Exodus, and the worship of the calf. It will also address the Prophet Muhammad directly, comforting him and reminding him of the purpose of revelation.\n\nFor the reader, the opening is a call to attention. You are about to hear the word of God. Set aside distractions. Listen carefully. The letters may be mysterious, but the message that follows is clear: God is one, the messengers are true, and the meeting with God is certain.\n\nThe opening also performs what the next verse will deny about the Book's purpose. Two syllables, no burden, no demand, and then immediately the reassurance that the Quran was not sent to exhaust the Prophet. The chapter that contains some of the hardest moments of Moses' story begins with a whisper rather than a weight, and the ordering is part of the mercy it announces.",
  "2": "\"We have not revealed the Quran to you O Prophet to cause you distress\" addresses the Prophet directly. The previous verse opened the chapter with the disconnected letters. This verse begins the message itself by reassuring the Prophet.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad faced enormous hardship. He was mocked, rejected, and persecuted. He grieved for his people and carried the weight of the message. The long nights of prayer and the constant opposition took a toll. God tells him that the Quran was not sent to add to his distress.\n\nThe word \"distress\" includes hardship, exhaustion, and sorrow. The Quran is not a burden designed to crush the Prophet. It is a guidance, a mercy, and a reminder. Its commands are meant to be manageable, and its weight is borne with God's help.\n\nThis verse also applies to all believers. The religion of Islam is not meant to be a source of misery. God does not desire hardship for His servants. He desires ease, as the Quran says in another verse: \"God intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship.\"\n\nThis does not mean the path is without difficulty. The prophets and believers were tested severely. But the religion itself, when understood and practiced correctly, brings peace rather than distress. Rigidity, extremism, and self-imposed burdens are not from Islam.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is an invitation to approach the Quran as a source of comfort, not distress. If following the religion has made you harsh, exhausted, or hopeless, you may be carrying burdens that God did not intend. Return to the Quran as it was meant to be: a reminder that leads to peace.\n\n'To cause you distress' also governs how the Prophet later taught others. He described the religion as easy and warned those who made it harsh, telling his envoys to give glad tidings and not to drive people away. A Book revealed without intent to exhaust its own messenger produces, in those who carry it correctly, the same temperament it was sent with.",
  "3": "\"but as a reminder to those in awe of Allah\" explains the true purpose of the Quran. The previous verse said it was not revealed to cause distress. This verse says what it was revealed for.\n\nThe Quran is a \"reminder.\" The Arabic word, \"dhikr,\" means something that brings to mind what is already known deep within. It reminds people of God, of their purpose, of right and wrong, and of the meeting with their Lord. It does not present entirely new information that human beings have no access to. It awakens what is buried under heedlessness.\n\nThe reminder is specifically for \"those in awe of Allah.\" The word for awe, \"khushyah,\" means a fear mixed with reverence and love. It is the kind of fear that makes a person careful about how they live. The Quran benefits those who approach it with this attitude.\n\nThis does not mean the Quran has no message for the disbelievers. It warns them and invites them. But its deepest benefit, its reminder, is received by those who come to it with awe. The person who reads it without any awareness of God may understand its words but will not be transformed by them.\n\nThe verse also implies that the Quran is not a book of abstract philosophy or academic theory. It is a living reminder for people who fear God and want to live according to His will. It speaks to their hearts, their struggles, and their hopes.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a description of how to approach the Quran. Come to it in awe of God. Seek to be reminded. Read it as a message meant for you, not as an object of study detached from your life. The one who reads with awe is the one who truly benefits.",
  "4": "\"It is a revelation from the One Who created the earth and the high heavens\" identifies the source of the Quran. The previous verse called it a reminder. This verse tells you who sent it.\n\nThe One who revealed the Quran is the same One who created the earth and the high heavens. He is not a local god, a tribal deity, or an abstract concept. He is the Creator of everything that exists. The authority of the Quran rests on the fact that it comes from the Maker of the universe.\n\nThe phrase \"high heavens\" points to the vastness and loftiness of the creation above us. The same God who made the farthest galaxies also speaks to human beings through the Quran. His greatness does not distance Him from His creatures. It is precisely because He is the Creator that He has the right to reveal and command.\n\nThe description of God as Creator also serves as an argument against those who rejected the Prophet. They could see the earth and the heavens. They knew they had not made themselves. The One who made them is the One who sent the Quran. To reject the message is to reject the One who created them.\n\nFor the reader, the verse anchors the Quran in the Creator. When you read it, you are not reading the words of a man. You are reading the word of the One who made you and everything around you. That fact should change how you listen.\n\n'The earth and the high heavens' also locates the revelation between the two places human beings actually occupy. They walk on the one and gaze at the other, and the verse names both as the work of the Speaker. The argument requires no library: look down, look up, and ask who could command the attention of the Maker of both. The Quran repeatedly grounds its authority in creation that any listener can verify with his own eyes.",
  "5": "\"the Most Compassionate, Who is established on the Throne\" continues the description of the Creator from the previous verse. The Quran was revealed by the One who made the earth and the high heavens. That same God is the Most Compassionate, established on the Throne.\n\nThe word \"established\" translates \"istawa,\" a term used in the Quran to describe God's being above the Throne. Muslims accept it as it has come, without asking how, without likening God to creation, and without denying it. The early scholars said that the establishment is known, its exact manner is unknown, and believing in it is obligatory.\n\nIt is important not to imagine God sitting on a physical throne like a human king. The Throne is the greatest of created things, and God is entirely distinct from His creation. His establishment on the Throne means that He is high above all things, that He governs all affairs, and that His sovereignty is complete.\n\nThe name Most Compassionate comes right after the mention of the Throne. This is meaningful. The King on the Throne is not a tyrant. He is the Most Merciful. His rule is not like the rule of earthly kings. It is a rule of wisdom, care, and compassion.\n\nFor the reader, the verse calls you to know God as both transcendent and merciful. He is above His creation, yet close to those who call on Him. He sits on the Throne, yet He is with you wherever you are. Hold both truths without reducing one to the other.\n\nThe Throne itself is described elsewhere in the Quran as extending beyond the heavens and the earth, which makes the mention here a scale-setter: the One whose seat exceeds the universe is the One speaking gently to a fugitive shepherd by a fire. Majesty and intimacy meet in a single verse, and neither is reduced to make room for the other.",
  "6": "\"To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth and whatever is in between and whatever is underground\" broadens the description of God's ownership. The previous verse said He is established on the Throne. This verse says everything belongs to Him.\n\nThe four categories cover everything that exists: what is in the heavens, what is on the earth, what lies between them, and what is hidden underground. No corner of creation is outside God's ownership and knowledge.\n\nThis includes things human beings cannot see. It includes the creatures of the deep oceans, the bacteria in the soil, the angels in the heavens, and the dead in their graves. All of them belong to God. He created them, He sustains them, and they return to Him.\n\nThe verse is a reminder that human ownership is temporary and limited. You may own a house, a piece of land, or a business, but the ultimate owner is God. You are a trustee, not a permanent owner. Everything you have belongs to Him and will one day return to Him.\n\nFor the reader, the verse is a call to humility. The God you worship owns everything. Nothing you have is truly yours. Hold what you have with gratitude and use it as He has commanded, knowing that the real Owner can take it back whenever He wills.\n\n'Whatever is underground' also reaches what no living person can inspect. Mines, bones, buried water, and whatever the earth conceals are named as owned, at a time when none of it could be surveyed. Ownership in the verse is not established by access; it is established by creation, and the list moves from the visible extremes to the invisible depths, as if the verse were walking its listener downward until sight gives out, and then declaring that the ownership continues.",
  "7": "\"Whether you speak openly or not, He certainly knows what is secret and what is even more hidden\" describes God's complete knowledge. The previous verse said everything belongs to Him. This verse says everything is known to Him.\n\nGod knows what is spoken openly. He also knows what is secret, the things you say behind closed doors or hide in your heart. And He knows what is \"even more hidden,\" thoughts that have not fully formed, secrets buried so deep that you may have forgotten them yourself.\n\nThere is no gradation of difficulty for God's knowledge. A thought you are aware of and a thought buried in your subconscious are equally known to Him. He does not learn, remember, or discover. He knows everything eternally and completely.\n\nThis verse is both a warning and a comfort. It is a warning because nothing you do or say or think is hidden from God. You cannot lie to Him or pretend to be someone you are not. It is a comfort because God knows your struggles, your pains, and your sincere intentions, even when other people do not see them.\n\nFor the reader, let this awareness shape your inner life. Be as careful with your thoughts as you are with your words, because God knows them both. And take comfort in the fact that the same God who sees your secrets also knows your heart and responds to your sincerity.\n\n'What is even more hidden' also has a practical edge. Secrets can be confessed, and hidden things can be discovered, but a motive half-understood by its owner cannot even be reported accurately. The verse reaches that deep, which means sincerity itself is safe with Him: a person's inarticulate intentions are better known to his Lord than to himself. Nowhere else can a human being be so completely understood.",
  "8": "\"Allah—there is no god worthy of worship except Him. He has the Most Beautiful Names\" brings the opening description of God to its conclusion. He is the Creator, the Owner, the All-Knower, and now He is declared to be the only One worthy of worship.\n\nThis is the core of Islam. Every prophet called to it. The Quran circles back to it again and again. The statement that there is no god but Allah is the foundation of a Muslim's life, the first thing said in the call to prayer, and the words uttered at death.\n\nThe second half of the verse says that God has the Most Beautiful Names. These are the names and attributes described in the Quran and the authentic teachings of the Prophet. They are the way human beings know who God is: the Most Merciful, the All-Knowing, the All-Powerful, the Forbearing, the Just.\n\nBelieving in God's names is not a matter of memorizing a list. It is a matter of letting those names shape your relationship with Him. You call on Him by them, you trust Him because of them, and you strive to reflect their meanings in your own character within human limits.\n\nFor the reader, this verse is the heart of the matter. Worship God alone. Know Him through His beautiful names. Build your life on this foundation, and everything else will find its proper place.\n\n'Most Beautiful Names' also sets the method for speaking about God. The Quran does not nickname its Lord; it names Him, and the names are given, not invented. Where scripture supplies the vocabulary, believers stay inside it, and where it is silent, they are silent. Revelation, not imagination, decides how the Maker is described. A servant who calls on the Merciful is calling on a name revealed from above, and the same is true when he calls on the Forgiving or the Hearing. The names turn prayer into an answered relationship rather than a shout into the dark.",
  "9": "“Has the story of Moses reached you ˹O Prophet˺?” opens the account, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse asks the question: “Has the story of Moses reached you O Prophet?” or “wa hal ataka hadeethu Moosa,” and the commentators note the question, for the Lord asked the Prophet whether the story of Moses had reached him, and the question is the introduction, the opening of the account that the following verses unfold, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the method, the drawing of the attention, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the story of the fire and the mission. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the introduction, and the story is the narrative, and the verse is the record of the opening, the introduction of the account of Moses.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own attention. The believer is taught that the story of Moses is the thing that the believer must know, and the one who attends to the narrative is the one whose attention the Lord values, and the believer who reads the story is the believer whose faith the story strengthens, and the question of the verse is the invitation, the call to the attention, and the believer who reads the opening is the believer whose attention the question sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the attention of the narrative: the question opened the story, and the believer who attends is the believer whose attention the Lord values, and the question is the teaching that the story is the thing that the believer must receive. The verse is the record of the opening, and the teaching of the attention that the narrative requires.",
  "10": "“When he saw a fire, he said to his family, “Wait here, ˹for˺ I have spotted a fire. Perhaps I can bring you a torch from it, or find some guidance at the fire.”” records the sighting, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the sighting: “When he saw a fire, he said to his family, ‘Wait here, for I have spotted a fire. Perhaps I can bring you a torch from it, or find some guidance at the fire,’” or “ith ra’a naran fa-qala li-ahlihi umkuthoo innee anastu naran la‘allee ateekum minha bi-qabasin aw ajidu ‘ala an-nari hudan,” and the commentators note the sighting, for Moses saw the fire and told the family to wait, and the hope of the torch or the guidance, and the verse is the record of the sighting, the step that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the fire is the sign, and the hope of the guidance is the prophecy, and the verse is the record of the sighting, the beginning of the call. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the fire is the sign, and the guidance is the call, and the verse is the record of the sighting, the step that the following verse reveals.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the seeking, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own striving. The believer is taught that the seeking of the means is the duty, and the one who seeks the fire is the one whom the Lord guides, and the believer who strives for the provision is the believer whose striving the Lord values, and the sighting of Moses is the model, the seeking of the means, and the believer who reads the sighting is the believer whose striving the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the seeking of the means: Moses sought the torch and found the guidance, and the believer who seeks the means is the believer whom the Lord guides, and the sighting is the teaching that the seeking is the path of the guidance. The verse is the record of the sighting, and the teaching of the striving that the seeking requires.",
  "11": "“But when he approached it, he was called, “O Moses!”” records the call, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the call: “But when he approached it, he was called, ‘O Moses!’” or “fa-lamma ataha noodiya ya Moosa,” and the commentators note the call, for the Lord called Moses as he approached the fire, and the call is the beginning of the revelation, the first word of the divine address, and the verse is the record of the call, the step that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the call by the name is the honour, and the verse is the record of the call, the beginning of the mission. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the call is the revelation, and the name is the honour, and the verse is the record of the call, the step that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own attention. The believer is taught that the call of the Lord is the honour, and the one who is called is the one whose mission the Lord grants, and the believer who attends to the call is the believer whose attention the Lord values, and the call of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the Lord addresses the chosen, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose attention the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honour of the call: Moses was called by the name, and the believer who attends to the call is the believer whom the Lord addresses, and the call is the teaching that the revelation is the honour. The verse is the record of the call, and the teaching of the attention that the call requires.",
  "12": "“It is truly I. I am your Lord! So take off your sandals, for you are in the sacred valley of Ṭuwa.”” records the first words, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the words: “It is truly I. I am your Lord! So take off your sandals, for you are in the sacred valley of Tuwa,” or “innee ana rabbuka fakhla‘ na‘layka innaka bil-wadi al-muqaddasi tuwan,” and the commentators note the words, for the Lord declared the identity and commanded the removal of the sandals, and the verse is the record of the first words, the declaration of the Lord and the command of the sanctity, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration \"I am your Lord\" is the introduction, and the command of the sandals is the reverence, and the verse is the record of the first words, the beginning of the revelation. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the declaration is the identity, and the command is the reverence, and the verse is the record of the first words, the declaration that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sanctity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reverence. The believer is taught that the presence of the Lord is the sanctity, and the one who is addressed is the one whose reverence the Lord values, and the believer who approaches the sacred is the believer whose reverence the Lord accepts, and the command of the sandals is the teaching, the declaration that the sacred place is the place of the reverence, and the believer who reads the words is the believer whose reverence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reverence of the presence: Moses removed the sandals, and the believer who approaches the sacred is the believer whose reverence the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the sanctity is the thing that the believer must honour. The verse is the record of the first words, and the teaching of the reverence that the presence requires.",
  "13": "“I have chosen you, so listen to what is revealed:” records the choosing, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the choosing: “I have chosen you, so listen to what is revealed,” or “wa ana ikhtartuka fastami‘ lima yooha,” and the commentators note the choosing, for the Lord declared the choosing of Moses, and the command of the listening, and the verse is the record of the choosing, the declaration that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the choosing is the election, and the listening is the attention, and the verse is the record of the choosing, the election of the prophet. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the choosing is the election, and the listening is the attention, and the verse is the record of the choosing, the election that the following verses display.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the choosing, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own attention. The believer is taught that the choosing of the Lord is the honour, and the one who is chosen is the one whose listening the Lord values, and the believer who attends to the revelation is the believer whose attention the Lord accepts, and the choosing of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the election is the path of the attention, and the believer who reads the choosing is the believer whose attention the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honour of the election: Moses was chosen, and the believer who attends to the revelation is the believer whom the Lord honours, and the choosing is the teaching that the listening is the duty. The verse is the record of the choosing, and the teaching of the attention that the election requires.",
  "14": "“‘It is truly I. I am Allah! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Me. So worship Me ˹alone˺, and establish prayer for My remembrance.”” records the creed, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the creed: “It is truly I. I am Allah! There is no god worthy of worship except Me. So worship Me alone, and establish prayer for My remembrance,” or “innanee ana Allahu la ilaha illa ana fa‘budnee wa aqimi as-salata li-thikree,” and the commentators note the creed, for the Lord declared the oneness and commanded the worship and the prayer, and the verse is the record of the creed, the foundation of the message, and the commentators note the significance, for the oneness is the first, and the prayer is the remembrance, and the verse is the record of the creed, the message that Moses would carry. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the oneness is the foundation, and the prayer is the remembrance, and the verse is the record of the creed, the message that the following verses develop.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the oneness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own worship. The believer is taught that the oneness is the foundation, and the one who worships the One is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who establishes the prayer is the believer whose remembrance the Lord accepts, and the creed of the Lord is the teaching, the declaration that the worship is the duty, and the believer who reads the creed is the believer whose worship the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the foundation of the message: the oneness and the prayer, and the believer who worships the One and establishes the prayer is the believer whose worship the Lord accepts, and the creed is the teaching that the remembrance is the end of the worship. The verse is the record of the creed, and the teaching of the worship that the oneness requires.",
  "15": "“The Hour is sure to come. My Will is to keep it hidden, so that every soul may be rewarded according to their efforts.”” records the Hour, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the Hour: “The Hour is sure to come. My Will is to keep it hidden, so that every soul may be rewarded according to their efforts,” or “inna as-sa‘ata atiyatun akadu ukhfeeha li-tujza kullu nafsin bima tas‘a,” and the commentators note the Hour, for the Lord declared the certainty of the Hour and the concealment of the time, and the verse is the record of the Hour, the declaration that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the certainty of the Hour is the warning, and the concealment of the time is the wisdom, and the verse is the record of the Hour, the teaching that the reckoning is the certain. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the Hour is the certain, and the concealment is the wisdom, and the verse is the record of the Hour, the declaration that the following verses develop.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the Hour, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own preparation. The believer is taught that the Hour is the certain, and the one who knows the certainty is the one whose preparation the Lord values, and the believer who lives with the knowledge of the reckoning is the believer whose effort the Lord accepts, and the declaration of the Hour is the teaching, the declaration that the reward is the measure of the effort, and the believer who reads the Hour is the believer whose preparation the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the Hour: the Hour is the sure, and the believer who lives with the knowledge is the believer whose effort the Lord rewards, and the concealment is the teaching that the preparation is the duty. The verse is the record of the Hour, and the teaching of the preparation that the certainty requires.",
  "16": "“So do not let those who disbelieve in it and follow their desires distract you from it, or you will be doomed.”” records the warning, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the warning: “So do not let those who disbelieve in it and follow their desires distract you from it, or you will be doomed,” or “fa-la yasuddannaka ‘anha man la yu’minu biha wattaba‘a hawahu fa-tarda,” and the commentators note the warning, for the Lord warned Moses against the distraction of the deniers, and the warning is the teaching, the declaration that the desire is the distraction, and the verse is the record of the warning, the caution that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the warning is the protection, and the distraction is the danger, and the verse is the record of the warning, the caution of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the warning is the protection, and the distraction is the desire, and the verse is the record of the warning, the caution that the following verses develop.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own steadfastness. The believer is taught that the desire is the distraction, and the one who is distracted is the one whose ruin the verse warns of, and the believer who holds the steadfastness is the believer whose focus the Lord values, and the warning of the Lord is the teaching, the declaration that the distraction is the danger, and the believer who reads the warning is the believer whose steadfastness the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the distraction: the desire is the ruin, and the believer who holds the focus is the believer whose steadfastness the Lord accepts, and the warning is the teaching that the distraction is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the warning, and the teaching of the steadfastness that the focus requires.",
  "17": "“˹Allah added,˺ “And what is that in your right hand, O Moses?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the question: “And what is that in your right hand, O Moses?” or “wa ma tilka bi-yameenika ya Moosa,” and the commentators note the question, for the Lord asked Moses about the staff, and the question is the method, the preparation of the miracle, and the verse is the record of the question, the step that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the introduction, the preparation of the sign, and the verse is the record of the question, the method of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the introduction, and the staff is the instrument, and the verse is the record of the question, the preparation of the miracle.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the question of the Lord is the preparation, and the one who is asked is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who attends to the signs is the believer whose attention the Lord accepts, and the question of the Lord is the teaching, the declaration that the ordinary is the instrument of the miracle, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the method of the Lord: the question prepared the miracle, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose attention the Lord values, and the question is the teaching that the ordinary is the vessel of the sign. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the reflection that the method invites.",
  "18": "“He replied, “It is my staff! I lean on it, and with it I beat down ˹branches˺ for my sheep, and have other uses for it.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “It is my staff! I lean on it, and with it I beat down branches for my sheep, and have other uses for it,” or “qala hiya ‘asaya atawakka’u ‘alayha wa ahushshu biha ‘ala ghanamee wa liya feeha maaribu ukhra,” and the commentators note the reply, for Moses described the staff, and the reply is the expansion, the listing of the uses, and the commentators note the detail, for the reply is the human, the lengthening of the answer, and the verse is the record of the reply, the description of the ordinary, and the commentators note the significance, for the reply is the preparation of the contrast, the ordinary that the following verse transforms. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the description, and the uses are the ordinary, and the verse is the record of the reply, the description that the following verse transforms.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own humility. The believer is taught that the ordinary is the vessel of the miracle, and the one who describes the ordinary is the one whose description the Lord transforms, and the believer who uses the means is the believer whose means the Lord honours, and the reply of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the staff is the ordinary, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the transformation of the ordinary: the staff was the ordinary, and the believer who uses the means is the believer whose means the Lord transforms, and the reply is the teaching that the ordinary is the vessel of the sign. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the humility that the transformation requires.",
  "19": "“Allah said, “Throw it down, O Moses!”” records the command, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the command: “Allah said, ‘Throw it down, O Moses!’” or “qala alqiha ya Moosa,” and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded Moses to throw the staff, and the command is the test, the obedience that the following verse rewards, and the verse is the record of the command, the step that the following verse fulfills, and the commentators note the significance, for the command is the test of the obedience, and the verse is the record of the command, the obedience that the following verse displays. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the test, and the throwing is the obedience, and the verse is the record of the command, the test that the following verse displays.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own obedience. The believer is taught that the command of the Lord is the test, and the one who obeys is the one whose obedience the Lord values, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the command to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the path of the miracle, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the obedience of the command: Moses threw the staff, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the obedience is the key of the sign. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the obedience that the command requires.",
  "20": "“So he did, then—behold!—it became a serpent, slithering.”” records the miracle, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the miracle: “So he did, then—behold!—it became a serpent, slithering,” or “fa-alqaha fa-itha hiya hayyatun tas‘a,” and the commentators note the miracle, for the staff became the serpent, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the first of the signs, and the commentators note the significance, for the transformation of the staff is the proof, and the slithering is the life, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the sign that the following verse commands the taking of. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the transformation is the miracle, and the slithering is the life, and the verse is the record of the miracle, the sign that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the miracle, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the miracle of the Lord is the proof, and the one who sees the sign is the one whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the transformation of the staff is the teaching, the declaration that the power of the Lord is the complete, and the believer who reads the miracle is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the staff became the serpent, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the miracle is the teaching that the transformation is the easy for the Lord. The verse is the record of the miracle, and the teaching of the certainty that the power produces.",
  "21": "“Allah said, “Take it, and have no fear. We will return it to its former state.”” records the reassurance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the reassurance: “Allah said, ‘Take it, and have no fear. We will return it to its former state,’” or “qala khuthha wa la takhaf sa-nu‘eeduha seerataha al-oola,” and the commentators note the reassurance, for the Lord commanded Moses to take the serpent and calmed the fear, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the reassurance is the comfort, and the return is the restoration, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reassurance is the comfort, and the return is the restoration, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reassurance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the reassurance of the Lord is the comfort, and the one who fears is the one whom the Lord calms, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the reassurance to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the thing that the Lord removes, and the believer who reads the reassurance is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the comfort of the Lord: Moses was calmed, and the believer who fears is the believer whom the Lord reassures, and the reassurance is the teaching that the trust is the remedy of the fear. The verse is the record of the reassurance, and the teaching of the trust that the comfort produces.",
  "22": "“And put your hand under your armpit, it will come out ˹shining˺ white, unblemished, as another sign,” records the second sign, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the sign: “And put your hand under your armpit, it will come out shining white, unblemished, as another sign,” or “wadmum yadaka ila janahika takhruj bayda’a min ghayri soo’in ayatan ukhra,” and the commentators note the sign, for the Lord commanded Moses to put the hand and the hand came out the white, and the verse is the record of the sign, the second of the signs, and the commentators note the significance, for the white hand is the light, and the unblemished is the purity, and the verse is the record of the sign, the second sign that the following verse gathers. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the hand is the sign, and the white is the light, and the verse is the record of the sign, the second sign that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sign, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the proofs, and the one who receives the signs is the one whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the white hand is the teaching, the declaration that the power of the Lord is the complete, and the believer who reads the sign is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the hand shone white, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the sign is the teaching that the light is the easy for the Lord. The verse is the record of the sign, and the teaching of the certainty that the power produces.",
  "23": "“so that We may show you some of Our greatest signs.”” completes the signs, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the signs: “so that We may show you some of Our greatest signs,” or “li-nuriyaka min ayatina al-kubra,” and the commentators note the completion, for the two signs are the portion, and the purpose is the showing of the greatest signs, and the verse is the record of the completion, the purpose that the following verse applies, and the commentators note the significance, for the two signs are the portion of the great, and the purpose is the preparation, and the verse is the record of the completion, the purpose of the signs. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the signs are the portion, and the purpose is the preparation, and the verse is the record of the completion, the purpose that the following verse fulfills.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the purpose, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the signs of the Lord are the preparation, and the one who receives the signs is the one whose certainty the Lord prepares, and the believer who reflects upon the signs is the believer whose certainty the Lord strengthens, and the purpose of the signs is the teaching, the declaration that the signs are the portion of the great, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the purpose of the signs: the signs prepared Moses, and the believer who reflects upon the signs is the believer whose certainty the Lord strengthens, and the completion is the teaching that the signs are the portion of the great. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the signs prepare.",
  "24": "“Go to Pharaoh, for he has truly transgressed ˹all bounds˺.”” records the mission, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the mission: “Go to Pharaoh, for he has truly transgressed all bounds,” or “ithhab ila Fir‘awna innahu tagha,” and the commentators note the mission, for the Lord commanded Moses to go to Pharaoh, and the description is the verdict, the \"tagha,\" the transgression, and the verse is the record of the mission, the command that the following verse answers with the prayer, and the commentators note the significance, for the mission is the charge, and the transgression is the cause, and the verse is the record of the mission, the command of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mission is the charge, and the transgression is the cause, and the verse is the record of the mission, the command that the following verses develop.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mission, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own duty. The believer is taught that the mission is the charge, and the one who is sent is the one whose duty the Lord values, and the believer who confronts the falsehood is the believer whose courage the Lord accepts, and the mission of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the transgression is the thing that the believer must confront, and the believer who reads the mission is the believer whose duty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the duty of the confrontation: Moses was sent to Pharaoh, and the believer who confronts the falsehood is the believer whose courage the Lord values, and the mission is the teaching that the transgression is the thing that the believer must face. The verse is the record of the mission, and the teaching of the duty that the confrontation requires.",
  "25": "“Moses prayed, “My Lord! Uplift my heart for me,”” records the first request, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the request: “My Lord! Uplift my heart for me,” or “qala rabbi ishrah lee sadree,” and the commentators note the request, for Moses asked the expansion of the chest, and the request is the first of the supplication, the seeking of the courage, and the verse is the record of the request, the first of the prayers that the following verses continue, and the commentators note the significance, for the expansion of the chest is the courage, and the verse is the record of the request, the first of the supplication of the mission. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the expansion is the courage, and the request is the seeking, and the verse is the record of the request, the first of the prayers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the request, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own courage. The believer is taught that the courage is the thing that the believer seeks, and the one who asks the expansion is the one whose courage the Lord grants, and the believer who prays for the strength is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the request of Moses is the model, the seeking of the courage, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose courage the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the seeking of the courage: Moses asked the expansion, and the believer who prays for the strength is the believer whose courage the Lord grants, and the request is the teaching that the courage is the gift. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the courage that the mission requires.",
  "26": "“and make my task easy,” records the second request, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the request: “and make my task easy,” or “wa yassir lee amree,” and the commentators note the request, for Moses asked the ease of the task, and the request is the second of the supplication, the seeking of the ease, and the verse is the record of the request, the second of the prayers, and the commentators note the significance, for the ease of the task is the help, and the verse is the record of the request, the second of the supplication. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the ease is the help, and the request is the seeking, and the verse is the record of the request, the second of the prayers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the ease, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the ease is the thing that the believer seeks, and the one who asks the ease is the one whose ease the Lord grants, and the believer who prays for the help is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the request of Moses is the model, the seeking of the ease, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose reliance the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the seeking of the ease: Moses asked the ease, and the believer who prays for the help is the believer whose ease the Lord grants, and the request is the teaching that the ease is the gift. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the reliance that the mission requires.",
  "27": "“and remove the impediment from my tongue” records the third request, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the request: “and remove the impediment from my tongue,” or “wahlul ‘uqdatan min lisanee,” and the commentators note the request, for Moses asked the removal of the knot from the tongue, and the request is the third of the supplication, the seeking of the eloquence, and the verse is the record of the request, the third of the prayers, and the commentators note the significance, for the knot of the tongue is the impediment, and the verse is the record of the request, the third of the supplication. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the knot is the impediment, and the request is the seeking, and the verse is the record of the request, the third of the prayers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the eloquence, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own speech. The believer is taught that the eloquence is the thing that the believer seeks, and the one who asks the clarity is the one whose clarity the Lord grants, and the believer who prays for the speech is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the request of Moses is the model, the seeking of the eloquence, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose speech the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the seeking of the eloquence: Moses asked the clarity, and the believer who prays for the speech is the believer whose clarity the Lord grants, and the request is the teaching that the eloquence is the gift. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the eloquence that the mission requires.",
  "28": "“so people may understand my speech,” completes the third request, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the request: “so people may understand my speech,” or “yafqahoo qawlee,” and the commentators note the completion, for the purpose of the removal is the understanding, and the verse is the record of the completion, the purpose that the following request continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the understanding is the end, and the verse is the record of the completion, the purpose of the eloquence. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the understanding is the end, and the completion is the purpose, and the verse is the record of the completion, the purpose that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the understanding, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own clarity. The believer is taught that the understanding is the end of the speech, and the one who seeks the clarity is the one whose speech the Lord uses, and the believer who speaks for the understanding is the believer whose speech the Lord values, and the completion of Moses is the model, the purpose of the eloquence, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose clarity the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the purpose of the speech: Moses sought the understanding, and the believer who speaks for the clarity is the believer whose speech the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the understanding is the end of the speech. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the clarity that the mission requires.",
  "29": "“and grant me a helper from my family,” records the fourth request, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the request: “and grant me a helper from my family,” or “waj‘al lee wazeeran min ahlee,” and the commentators note the request, for Moses asked the helper from the family, and the request is the fourth of the supplication, the seeking of the support, and the verse is the record of the request, the fourth of the prayers, and the commentators note the significance, for the helper is the support, and the verse is the record of the request, the fourth of the supplication. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the helper is the support, and the request is the seeking, and the verse is the record of the request, the fourth of the prayers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the support, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the support is the thing that the believer seeks, and the one who asks the helper is the one whose support the Lord grants, and the believer who prays for the companion is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the request of Moses is the model, the seeking of the support, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose reliance the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the seeking of the support: Moses asked the helper, and the believer who prays for the companion is the believer whose support the Lord grants, and the request is the teaching that the support is the gift. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the reliance that the mission requires.",
  "30": "“Aaron, my brother.”” specifies the helper, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse specifies the helper: “Aaron, my brother,” or “Haroona akhee,” and the commentators note the specification, for Moses named Aaron, and the naming is the request, the specification of the helper, and the verse is the record of the specification, the request that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the naming of Aaron is the prayer, and the verse is the record of the specification, the request of the brother. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the naming is the specification, and the brother is the helper, and the verse is the record of the specification, the request that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the specification, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own prayer. The believer is taught that the specific prayer is the sincere, and the one who names the need is the one whose prayer the Lord hears, and the believer who prays with the specification is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the specification of Moses is the model, the naming of the need, and the believer who reads the specification is the believer whose prayer the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the sincerity of the prayer: Moses named Aaron, and the believer who prays with the specification is the believer whose prayer the Lord values, and the naming is the teaching that the specific is the sincere. The verse is the record of the specification, and the teaching of the sincerity that the prayer requires.",
  "31": "“Strengthen me through him,” records the fifth request, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the request: “Strengthen me through him,” or “ushdud bihi azree,” and the commentators note the request, for Moses asked the strengthening through Aaron, and the request is the fifth of the supplication, the seeking of the strength, and the verse is the record of the request, the fifth of the prayers, and the commentators note the significance, for the strengthening is the support, and the verse is the record of the request, the fifth of the supplication. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the strengthening is the support, and the request is the seeking, and the verse is the record of the request, the fifth of the prayers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the strengthening, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the strengthening is the thing that the believer seeks, and the one who asks the strength is the one whose strength the Lord grants, and the believer who prays for the support is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the request of Moses is the model, the seeking of the strength, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose reliance the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the seeking of the strength: Moses asked the strengthening, and the believer who prays for the support is the believer whose strength the Lord grants, and the request is the teaching that the strength is the gift. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the reliance that the mission requires.",
  "32": "“and let him share my task,” records the sixth request, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the request: “and let him share my task,” or “wa ashrik-hu fee amree,” and the commentators note the request, for Moses asked the sharing of the task, and the request is the sixth of the supplication, the seeking of the partnership, and the verse is the record of the request, the sixth of the prayers, and the commentators note the significance, for the sharing is the partnership, and the verse is the record of the request, the sixth of the supplication. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the sharing is the partnership, and the request is the seeking, and the verse is the record of the request, the sixth of the prayers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the partnership, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the partnership is the thing that the believer seeks, and the one who asks the sharing is the one whose partnership the Lord grants, and the believer who prays for the companion is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the request of Moses is the model, the seeking of the partnership, and the believer who reads the request is the believer whose reliance the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the seeking of the partnership: Moses asked the sharing, and the believer who prays for the companion is the believer whose partnership the Lord grants, and the request is the teaching that the sharing is the gift. The verse is the record of the request, and the teaching of the reliance that the mission requires.",
  "33": "“so that we may glorify You much” records the purpose, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the purpose: “so that we may glorify You much,” or “kay nusabbihaka katheeran,” and the commentators note the purpose, for the partnership is the glorification, and the verse is the record of the purpose, the first of the two purposes, and the commentators note the significance, for the glorification is the end, and the verse is the record of the purpose, the first of the two. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the glorification is the end, and the purpose is the worship, and the verse is the record of the purpose, the first of the two purposes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the glorification, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own worship. The believer is taught that the glorification is the end of the support, and the one who seeks the help is the one whose help the Lord directs to the worship, and the believer who glorifies the Lord is the believer whose worship the Lord values, and the purpose of Moses is the model, the seeking of the glorification, and the believer who reads the purpose is the believer whose worship the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the support: Moses sought the glorification, and the believer who seeks the help is the believer whose help the Lord directs to the worship, and the purpose is the teaching that the glorification is the end. The verse is the record of the purpose, and the teaching of the worship that the support serves.",
  "34": "“and remember You much,” completes the purpose, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the purpose: “and remember You much,” or “wa nathkuraka katheeran,” and the commentators note the completion, for the remembrance is the second purpose, and the verse is the record of the completion, the second of the two purposes, and the commentators note the significance, for the remembrance is the end, and the verse is the record of the completion, the second of the two. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the remembrance is the end, and the completion is the worship, and the verse is the record of the completion, the second of the two purposes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the remembrance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own worship. The believer is taught that the remembrance is the end of the support, and the one who seeks the help is the one whose help the Lord directs to the remembrance, and the believer who remembers the Lord is the believer whose worship the Lord values, and the completion of Moses is the model, the seeking of the remembrance, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose worship the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the support: Moses sought the remembrance, and the believer who seeks the help is the believer whose help the Lord directs to the worship, and the completion is the teaching that the remembrance is the end. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the worship that the support serves.",
  "35": "“for truly You have ˹always˺ been overseeing us.”” closes the supplication, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse closes the supplication: “for truly You have always been overseeing us,” or “innaka kunta bina baseeran,” and the commentators note the closing, for the praise of the oversight is the conclusion, and the verse is the record of the closing, the praise that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the oversight is the attribute, and the verse is the record of the closing, the praise of the seer. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the oversight is the attribute, and the praise is the conclusion, and the verse is the record of the closing, the praise that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the oversight, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the oversight of the Lord is the comfort, and the one who knows the sight is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who lives with the knowledge of the oversight is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the closing of Moses is the model, the praise of the seer, and the believer who reads the closing is the believer whose trust the model sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the oversight of the Lord: Moses praised the seer, and the believer who lives with the knowledge of the sight is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the closing is the teaching that the oversight is the comfort. The verse is the record of the closing, and the teaching of the trust that the oversight produces.",
  "36": "“Allah responded, “All that you requested has been granted, O Moses!”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the answer: “All that you requested has been granted, O Moses!” or “qala qad ooteeta su’laka ya Moosa,” and the commentators note the answer, for the Lord granted all the requests, and the verse is the record of the answer, the grant that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the grant of all is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the grant is the mercy, and the answer is the acceptance, and the verse is the record of the answer, the grant that the following verses display.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the answer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the prayer of the believer is the answered, and the one who asks the Lord is the one whom the Lord grants, and the believer who prays with the sincerity is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the answer to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the supplication is the accepted, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the prayer: Moses was granted all, and the believer who prays with the sincerity is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the answer is the teaching that the supplication is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the hope that the answer carries.",
  "37": "““And surely We had shown You favour before,” records the reminder, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the reminder: “And surely We had shown You favour before,” or “wa laqad mananna ‘alayka marratan ukhra,” and the commentators note the reminder, for the Lord reminded Moses of the earlier favour, and the reminder is the introduction, the recollection of the past grace, and the verse is the record of the reminder, the step that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the earlier favour is the first grace, and the verse is the record of the reminder, the introduction of the recollection. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the favour is the grace, and the reminder is the recollection, and the verse is the record of the reminder, the step that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reminder, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the remembrance of the favour is the gratitude, and the one who recalls the grace is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who remembers the favours is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the reminder to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the favour is the thing that the believer must recall, and the believer who reads the reminder is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gratitude of the favour: Moses was reminded, and the believer who recalls the grace is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the reminder is the teaching that the favour is the thing that the believer must remember. The verse is the record of the reminder, and the teaching of the gratitude that the favour requires.",
  "38": "“when We inspired your mother with this:”” records the inspiration, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the inspiration: “when We inspired your mother with this,” or “ith awhayna ila ummika ma yooha,” and the commentators note the inspiration, for the Lord inspired the mother of Moses, and the verse is the record of the inspiration, the step that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the inspiration is the guidance, and the verse is the record of the inspiration, the step that the following verse completes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the inspiration is the guidance, and the mother is the vessel, and the verse is the record of the inspiration, the step that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the inspiration, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the inspiration of the Lord is the guidance, and the one who receives the guidance is the one whose affair the Lord arranges, and the believer who trusts the guidance is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the inspiration to the mother is the teaching, the declaration that the Lord guides the affairs, and the believer who reads the inspiration is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the guidance of the Lord: the mother was inspired, and the believer who trusts the guidance is the believer whose affair the Lord arranges, and the inspiration is the teaching that the Lord guides the affairs. The verse is the record of the inspiration, and the teaching of the trust that the guidance produces.",
  "39": "“‘Put him into a chest, then put it into the river. The river will wash it ashore, and he will be taken by ˹Pharaoh,˺ an enemy of Mine and his.’ And I blessed you with lovability from Me ˹O Moses˺ so that you would be brought up under My ˹watchful˺ Eye.”” records the instruction, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the instruction: “Put him into a chest, then put it into the river… And I blessed you with lovability from Me O Moses so that you would be brought up under My watchful Eye,” or “ani iqthifeehi fi at-tabooti faqthifeehi fi al-yammi… wa alqaytu ‘alayka mahabbatan minnee wa li-tusna‘a ‘ala ‘aynee,” and the commentators note the instruction, for the mother was commanded to cast the child, and the blessing of the lovability, and the verse is the record of the instruction, the guidance that saved Moses, and the commentators note the significance, for the casting of the child is the trust, and the lovability is the gift, and the verse is the record of the instruction, the guidance that the following verse completes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the instruction is the trust, and the lovability is the gift, and the verse is the record of the instruction, the guidance of the Lord.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the trust, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the trust of the Lord is the thing that saves, and the one who casts the affair to the Lord is the one whose affair the Lord arranges, and the believer who trusts the guidance is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the instruction to the mother is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the path of the safety, and the believer who reads the instruction is the believer whose reliance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the mother: the child was cast and saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose affair the Lord arranges, and the instruction is the teaching that the trust is the safety. The verse is the record of the instruction, and the teaching of the reliance that the trust requires.",
  "40": "“˹Remember˺ when your sister came along and proposed, ‘Shall I direct you to someone who will nurse him?’ So We reunited you with your mother so that her heart would be put at ease, and she would not grieve. ˹Later˺ you killed a man ˹by mistake˺, but We saved you from sorrow, as well as other tests We put you through. Then you stayed for a number of years among the people of Midian. Then you came here as pre-destined, O Moses!”” records the recollection, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the recollection: “Remember when your sister came along… Then you came here as pre-destined, O Moses!” or “ith tamshee ukhtuka… thumma ji’ta ‘ala qadarin ya Moosa,” and the commentators note the recollection, for the Lord recounted the sister and the mother and the killing and the Midian, and the verse is the record of the recollection, the summary of the past, and the commentators note the significance, for the recollection is the gratitude, the recounting of the favours, and the verse is the record of the recollection, the summary that the following verse closes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the recollection is the gratitude, and the favours are the mercies, and the verse is the record of the recollection, the summary of the grace.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the recollection, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the recollection of the favours is the gratitude, and the one who recounts the grace is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who remembers the mercies is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the recollection of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the favours are the many, and the believer who reads the recollection is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gratitude of the favours: Moses was reminded of the many, and the believer who recalls the grace is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the recollection is the teaching that the favours are the mercies. The verse is the record of the recollection, and the teaching of the gratitude that the favours require.",
  "41": "“And I have selected you for My service.”” records the selection, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the selection: “And I have selected you for My service,” or “wastana‘tuka li-nafsee,” and the commentators note the selection, for the Lord declared the selection of Moses, and the verse is the record of the selection, the purpose of the recollection, and the commentators note the significance, for the selection is the honour, and the verse is the record of the selection, the purpose that the following verse applies. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the selection is the honour, and the service is the mission, and the verse is the record of the selection, the purpose that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the selection, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own sincerity. The believer is taught that the selection of the Lord is the honour, and the one whom the Lord selects is the one whose service the Lord values, and the believer who lives the sincerity is the believer whose sincerity the Lord accepts, and the selection of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the service is the purpose, and the believer who reads the selection is the believer whose sincerity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honour of the selection: Moses was selected, and the believer who lives the sincerity is the believer whom the Lord selects, and the selection is the teaching that the service is the purpose. The verse is the record of the selection, and the teaching of the sincerity that the service requires.",
  "42": "““Go forth, you and your brother, with My signs and never falter in remembering Me.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the command: “Go forth, you and your brother, with My signs and never falter in remembering Me,” or “ithhab anta wa akhooka bi-ayatee wa la taniya fee thikree,” and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded the two brothers to go, and the command is the charge, the sending of the mission, and the verse is the record of the command, the charge that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the signs are the proofs, and the remembrance is the duty, and the verse is the record of the command, the charge of the mission. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the charge, and the signs are the proofs, and the verse is the record of the command, the charge that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own remembrance. The believer is taught that the remembrance of the Lord is the duty, and the one who remembers is the one whose remembrance the Lord values, and the believer who persists in the remembrance is the believer whose constancy the Lord accepts, and the command to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the remembrance is the thing that the believer must never abandon, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose remembrance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the remembrance: the command joined the signs and the remembrance, and the believer who persists in the remembrance is the believer whose constancy the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the remembrance is the companion of the mission. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the remembrance that the mission requires.",
  "43": "““Go, both of you, to Pharaoh, for he has truly transgressed ˹all bounds˺.”” repeats the mission, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse repeats the mission: “Go, both of you, to Pharaoh, for he has truly transgressed all bounds,” or “ithhaba ila Fir‘awna innahu tagha,” and the commentators note the mission, for the Lord repeated the command, and the repetition is the emphasis, the confirmation of the charge, and the verse is the record of the mission, the charge that the following verse qualifies, and the commentators note the significance, for the transgression is the cause, and the verse is the record of the mission, the charge of the confrontation. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mission is the charge, and the transgression is the cause, and the verse is the record of the mission, the charge that the following verse qualifies.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mission, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own courage. The believer is taught that the confrontation of the falsehood is the duty, and the one who faces the tyrant is the one whose courage the Lord values, and the believer who confronts the transgression is the believer whose courage the Lord accepts, and the mission of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the transgression is the thing that the believer must face, and the believer who reads the mission is the believer whose courage the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the duty of the confrontation: Moses was sent to the tyrant, and the believer who faces the falsehood is the believer whose courage the Lord values, and the mission is the teaching that the transgression is the thing that the believer must confront. The verse is the record of the mission, and the teaching of the courage that the confrontation requires.",
  "44": "““Speak to him gently, so perhaps he may be mindful ˹of Me˺ or fearful ˹of My punishment˺.”” records the instruction, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the instruction: “Speak to him gently, so perhaps he may be mindful of Me or fearful of My punishment,” or “fa-qoola lahu qawlan layyinan la‘allahu yatathakkaru aw yakhsha,” and the commentators note the instruction, for the Lord commanded the gentle speech, and the instruction is the wisdom, the declaration that the gentleness is the method, and the verse is the record of the instruction, the wisdom that the following verse applies, and the commentators note the significance, for the gentle speech to the tyrant is the highest of the manners, and the hope of the mindfulness and the fear is the purpose, and the verse is the record of the instruction, the wisdom of the mission. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the gentleness is the method, and the purpose is the return, and the verse is the record of the instruction, the wisdom that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the gentleness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own calling. The believer is taught that the gentleness is the method, and the one who speaks with the gentleness is the one whose calling the Lord values, and the believer who hopes the return of the wrongdoer is the believer whose mercy the Lord accepts, and the instruction to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the gentle speech is the path of the calling, and the believer who reads the instruction is the believer whose gentleness the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gentleness of the calling: Moses was commanded the gentleness, and the believer who calls with the mercy is the believer whose calling the Lord values, and the instruction is the teaching that the gentleness is the method of the truth. The verse is the record of the instruction, and the teaching of the gentleness that the calling requires.",
  "45": "“They both pleaded, “Our Lord! We fear that he may be quick to harm us or act tyrannically.”” records the plea, and the commentators note the two parts of the plea. The verse records the plea: “They both pleaded, ‘Our Lord! We fear that he may be quick to harm us or act tyrannically,’” or “qala rabbana innana nakhafu an yafruta ‘alayna aw an yatgha,” and the commentators note the plea, for the two brothers expressed the fear, and the plea is the human, the confession of the fear, and the verse is the record of the plea, the fear that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the plea is the human, and the fear is the thing that the reassurance would remove, and the verse is the record of the plea, the fear of the two. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the plea is the human, and the fear is the thing that the following verse calms.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plea, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the fear of the trial is the human, and the one who confesses the fear is the one whose fear the Lord calms, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the fear is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the plea of the two is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the thing that the Lord removes, and the believer who reads the plea is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humanity of the fear: the two feared and were reassured, and the believer who confesses the fear is the believer whose fear the Lord calms, and the plea is the teaching that the trust is the remedy of the fear. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the trust that the reassurance produces.",
  "46": "“Allah reassured ˹them˺, “Have no fear! I am with you, hearing and seeing.”” records the reassurance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the reassurance: “Have no fear! I am with you, hearing and seeing,” or “qala la takhafa innanee ma‘akuma asma‘u wa ara,” and the commentators note the reassurance, for the Lord calmed the fear and declared the presence, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the presence is the protection, and the hearing and the seeing are the two attributes, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the presence is the protection, and the hearing and the seeing are the attributes, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reassurance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the presence of the Lord is the protection, and the one who knows the presence is the one whose fear the Lord removes, and the believer who trusts the presence is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the reassurance to the two is the teaching, the declaration that the presence is the safety, and the believer who reads the reassurance is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the presence of the Lord: the two were reassured, and the believer who trusts the presence is the believer whose fear the Lord removes, and the reassurance is the teaching that the presence is the protection. The verse is the record of the reassurance, and the teaching of the trust that the presence produces.",
  "47": "““So go to him and say, ‘Indeed we are both messengers from your Lord, so let the Children of Israel go with us, and do not oppress them. We have come to you with a sign from your Lord. And salvation will be for whoever follows the ˹right˺ guidance.”” records the message, and the commentators note the two parts of the message. The verse records the message: “So go to him and say, ‘Indeed we are both messengers from your Lord, so let the Children of Israel go with us…’” or “fa’tiyahu fa-qoola inna rasoola rabbika fa-arsil ma‘ana Banee Isra’eela wa la tu‘aththibhum qad ji’naka bi-ayatin min rabbika was-salamu ‘ala mani ittaba‘a al-huda,” and the commentators note the message, for the Lord dictated the address to Pharaoh, and the message is the declaration, the demand of the release and the warning, and the verse is the record of the message, the address that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the message is the call, and the demand is the release, and the verse is the record of the message, the address of the mission. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the message is the call, and the demand is the release, and the verse is the record of the message, the address that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the message, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own calling. The believer is taught that the message of the truth is the declaration, and the one who delivers the message is the one whose delivery the Lord values, and the believer who calls to the guidance is the believer whose call the Lord accepts, and the message to Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the believer must deliver, and the believer who reads the message is the believer whose calling the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the delivery of the message: Moses was given the address, and the believer who delivers the truth is the believer whose delivery the Lord values, and the message is the teaching that the guidance is the thing that the believer must offer. The verse is the record of the message, and the teaching of the calling that the mission requires.",
  "48": "““It has indeed been revealed to us that the punishment will be upon whoever denies ˹the truth˺ and turns away.’”” completes the message, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the message: “It has indeed been revealed to us that the punishment will be upon whoever denies the truth and turns away,” or “inna qad oohiya ilayna anna al-‘athaba ‘ala man kaththaba wa tawalla,” and the commentators note the completion, for the message ends with the warning, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the warning is the closing, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning of the mission. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the warning is the closing, and the completion is the message, and the verse is the record of the completion, the warning that the following verse receives.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the punishment of the denial is the certain, and the one who denies is the one whom the warning finds, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion of the message is the teaching, the declaration that the warning is the thing that the believer must deliver, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the warning: the punishment is the revealed, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the warning is the closing of the message. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the warning produces.",
  "49": "“Pharaoh asked, “Who then is the Lord of you two, O Moses?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the question: “Who then is the Lord of you two, O Moses?” or “qala fa-man rabbukuma ya Moosa,” and the commentators note the question, for Pharaoh asked of the Lord, and the question is the confrontation, the opening of the debate, and the verse is the record of the question, the step that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the test, and the verse is the record of the question, the opening of the dialogue. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the test, and the answer is the truth, and the verse is the record of the question, the opening of the debate.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own answer. The believer is taught that the question of the falsehood is the occasion of the truth, and the one who is asked is the one whose answer the Lord guides, and the believer who answers with the truth is the believer whose answer the Lord values, and the question of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the question is the door of the answer, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose answer the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the occasion of the question: Pharaoh asked and Moses answered, and the believer who answers with the truth is the believer whose answer the Lord guides, and the question is the teaching that the inquiry is the door of the truth. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the answer that the question invites.",
  "50": "“He answered, “Our Lord is the One Who has given everything its ˹distinctive˺ form, then guided ˹it˺.”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The verse records the answer: “Our Lord is the One Who has given everything its distinctive form, then guided it,” or “qala rabbuna allathee a‘ta kulla shay’in khalqahu thumma hada,” and the commentators note the answer, for Moses declared the Lord who gives the form and the guidance, and the answer is the argument, the declaration of the creation and the guidance, and the verse is the record of the answer, the argument that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the giving of the form is the creation, and the guidance is the direction, and the verse is the record of the answer, the argument of the truth. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the form is the creation, and the guidance is the direction, and the verse is the record of the answer, the argument that the following verses develop.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the answer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the creation and the guidance are the proof, and the one who declares the proof is the one whose argument the Lord values, and the believer who knows the Lord is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the answer of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the Lord is the giver of the form and the guidance, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the proof of the creation: Moses declared the form and the guidance, and the believer who knows the proof is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the answer is the teaching that the creation is the evidence. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the certainty that the proof produces.",
  "51": "“Pharaoh asked, “And what about previous peoples?”” records the second question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the question: “And what about previous peoples?” or “qala fa-ma balu al-qurooni al-oola,” and the commentators note the question, for Pharaoh asked of the earlier generations, and the question is the attempt, the shifting of the debate, and the verse is the record of the question, the step that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the diversion, and the verse is the record of the question, the attempt of the tyrant. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the diversion, and the answer is the truth, and the verse is the record of the question, the attempt that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the question of the past is the attempt to divert, and the one who is asked is the one whose answer the Lord guides, and the believer who answers with the truth is the believer whose answer the Lord values, and the question of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the diversion is the method of the falsehood, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer to the diversion: Pharaoh asked of the past, and the believer who answers with the truth is the believer whose answer the Lord guides, and the question is the teaching that the diversion is the thing that the truth answers. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the certainty that the answer requires.",
  "52": "“He replied, “That knowledge is with my Lord in a Record. My Lord neither falters nor forgets ˹anything˺.”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The verse records the answer: “That knowledge is with my Lord in a Record. My Lord neither falters nor forgets anything,” or “qala ‘ilmuha ‘inda rabbee fee kitabin la yadillu rabbee wa la yansa,” and the commentators note the answer, for Moses attributed the knowledge of the past to the Lord, and the answer is the teaching, the declaration that the knowledge of the Lord is the complete, and the verse is the record of the answer, the attribution that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the Record is the knowledge, and the absence of the faltering and the forgetting is the perfection, and the verse is the record of the answer, the attribution of the knowledge. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the Record is the knowledge, and the perfection is the attribute, and the verse is the record of the answer, the attribution that the following verses develop.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the knowledge, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the knowledge of the Lord is the complete, and the one who attributes the knowledge is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the knowledge is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the answer of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the knowledge is the Lord's, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the perfection of the knowledge: Moses attributed the past to the Lord, and the believer who trusts the knowledge is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the answer is the teaching that the Lord neither falters nor forgets. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the certainty that the knowledge produces.",
  "53": "“˹He is the One˺ Who has laid out the earth for ˹all of˺ you, and set in it pathways for you, and sends down rain from the sky, causing various types of plants to grow,” records the signs, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the signs: “He is the One Who has laid out the earth for all of you, and set in it pathways for you, and sends down rain from the sky, causing various types of plants to grow,” or “allathee ja‘ala lakumu al-arda mahdan wa salaka lakum feeha subulan wa anzala mina as-sama’i ma’an fa-akhrajna bihi azwajan min nabatin shatta,” and the commentators note the signs, for Moses declared the earth and the paths and the rain and the plants, and the verse is the record of the signs, the argument of the creation, and the commentators note the significance, for the four signs are the proofs, and the verse is the record of the signs, the argument that the following verse completes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the earth and the paths and the rain and the plants are the signs, and the verse is the record of the signs, the argument of the creation.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the signs, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the signs of the creation are the proofs, and the one who reflects upon the signs is the one whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the believer who reads the creation is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the signs of Moses are the teaching, the declaration that the creation is the evidence, and the believer who reads the signs is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the proofs of the creation: Moses declared the signs, and the believer who reflects upon the creation is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the signs are the teaching that the creation is the evidence. The verse is the record of the signs, and the teaching of the certainty that the creation produces.",
  "54": "“˹so˺ eat and graze your cattle. Surely in this are signs for people of sound judgment.”” completes the signs, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the signs: “so eat and graze your cattle. Surely in this are signs for people of sound judgment,” or “kuloo war‘aw an‘amakum inna fee thalika la-ayatin li-uli an-nuha,” and the commentators note the completion, for the provision is the benefit, and the signs are for the people of the intellect, and the verse is the record of the completion, the teaching that the creation serves the creation, and the commentators note the significance, for the eating and the grazing are the benefits, and the signs are for the \"uli an-nuha,\" the people of the sound judgment, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument that the following verse develops. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the provision is the benefit, and the signs are for the intellect, and the verse is the record of the completion, the argument of the creation.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the judgment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the signs of the creation are for the intellect, and the one who reflects with the judgment is the one whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the believer who reads the creation with the mind is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the completion of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the duty, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reflection of the creation: the signs are for the judgment, and the believer who reflects with the mind is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the reflection is the key of the certainty. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the reflection that the creation invites.",
  "55": "“From the earth We created you, and into it We will return you, and from it We will bring you back again.”” records the cycle, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the cycle: “From the earth We created you, and into it We will return you, and from it We will bring you back again,” or “minha khalaqnakum wa feeha nu‘eedukum wa minha nukhrijukum taratan ukhra,” and the commentators note the cycle, for the Lord declared the three stages, the creation and the return and the resurrection, and the verse is the record of the cycle, the argument of the resurrection, and the commentators note the significance, for the three stages are the proof, and the verse is the record of the cycle, the argument that the following verse applies. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the three stages are the proof, and the cycle is the argument, and the verse is the record of the cycle, the argument of the resurrection.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the cycle, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the cycle of the creation is the proof of the resurrection, and the one who reflects upon the three stages is the one whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the cycle of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the return is the certain, and the believer who reads the cycle is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the proof of the resurrection: the three stages are the argument, and the believer who reflects upon the cycle is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the cycle is the teaching that the resurrection is the certain. The verse is the record of the cycle, and the teaching of the certainty that the proof produces.",
  "56": "“And We certainly showed Pharaoh all of Our signs, but he denied them and refused ˹to believe˺.”” records the rejection, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the rejection: “And We certainly showed Pharaoh all of Our signs, but he denied them and refused to believe,” or “wa laqad araynahu ayatina kullaha fa-kaththaba wa aba,” and the commentators note the rejection, for the Lord showed all the signs and Pharaoh denied, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the denial that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the denial of the signs after the showing is the gravest of the sins, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the denial of the tyrant. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the signs are the proofs, and the denial is the refusal, and the verse is the record of the rejection, the denial that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the rejection of the signs is the refusal, and the one who denies the proof is the one whose denial the Lord judges, and the believer who accepts the signs is the believer whose acceptance the Lord values, and the rejection of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the thing that the proof cannot cure, and the believer who reads the rejection is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the denial: Pharaoh saw all and denied, and the believer who accepts the signs is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the rejection is the teaching that the denial is the will, not the evidence. The verse is the record of the rejection, and the teaching of the certainty that the acceptance requires.",
  "57": "“He said, “Have you come to drive us out of our land with your magic, O Moses?”” records the accusation, and the commentators note the two parts of the accusation. The verse records the accusation: “Have you come to drive us out of our land with your magic, O Moses?” or “qala a-ji’tana li-tukhrijana min ardina bi-sihrika ya Moosa,” and the commentators note the accusation, for Pharaoh accused Moses of the magic, and the accusation is the evasion, the label of the truth as the sorcery, and the verse is the record of the accusation, the label that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the accusation is the oldest of the rejecters' weapons, and the verse is the record of the accusation, the evasion of the tyrant. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the accusation is the evasion, and the label is the refusal, and the verse is the record of the accusation, the evasion that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the accusation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the accusation of the magic is the evasion, and the one who labels the truth is the one whose label the Lord exposes, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the accusation of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the label is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the accusation is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the label: Pharaoh called the truth the magic, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the accusation is the teaching that the label is the refuge of the falsehood. The verse is the record of the accusation, and the teaching of the certainty that the label cannot shake.",
  "58": "““We can surely meet you with similar magic. So set for us an appointment that neither of us will fail to keep, in a central place.”” records the challenge, and the commentators note the two parts of the challenge. The verse records the challenge: “We can surely meet you with similar magic. So set for us an appointment that neither of us will fail to keep, in a central place,” or “fa-la-na’tiyannaka bi-sihrin mithlihi faj‘al baynana wa baynaka maw‘idan la nukhlifuhu nahnu wa la anta makanan suwan,” and the commentators note the challenge, for Pharaoh proposed the meeting of the magic, and the challenge is the confidence, the claim of the matching, and the verse is the record of the challenge, the proposal that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the challenge is the confidence of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the challenge, the proposal of the contest. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the challenge is the confidence, and the proposal is the contest, and the verse is the record of the challenge, the proposal that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the challenge, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own confidence. The believer is taught that the challenge of the falsehood is the opportunity, and the one who is challenged is the one whose truth the Lord displays, and the believer who faces the challenge is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the challenge of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the challenge is the occasion of the truth, and the believer who reads the challenge is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the occasion of the challenge: Pharaoh challenged and lost, and the believer who faces the challenge is the believer whose truth the Lord displays, and the challenge is the teaching that the confidence of the falsehood is the prelude of its fall. The verse is the record of the challenge, and the teaching of the confidence that the truth supplies.",
  "59": "“Moses said, “Your appointment is on the Day of the Festival, and let the people be gathered mid-morning.”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The verse records the answer: “Your appointment is on the Day of the Festival, and let the people be gathered mid-morning,” or “qala maw‘idukum yawmu az-zeenati wa an yuhshara an-nasu duhan,” and the commentators note the answer, for Moses set the day of the festival, and the answer is the wisdom, the choice of the public day, and the verse is the record of the answer, the wisdom that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the day of the festival is the public, and the gathering is the witness, and the verse is the record of the answer, the wisdom of the truth. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the day is the public, and the gathering is the witness, and the verse is the record of the answer, the wisdom that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the wisdom, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own confidence. The believer is taught that the truth seeks the public, and the one who chooses the witness is the one whose truth the Lord displays, and the believer who faces the falsehood openly is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the answer of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the public is the arena of the truth, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the openness of the truth: Moses chose the public day, and the believer who faces the falsehood openly is the believer whose truth the Lord displays, and the answer is the teaching that the truth does not fear the witness. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the confidence that the truth supplies.",
  "60": "“Pharaoh then withdrew, orchestrated his scheme, then returned.”” records the withdrawal, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the withdrawal: “Pharaoh then withdrew, orchestrated his scheme, then returned,” or “fa-tawalla Fir‘awnu fa-jama‘a kaydahu thumma ata,” and the commentators note the withdrawal, for Pharaoh withdrew and gathered the scheme, and the verse is the record of the withdrawal, the gathering of the magicians, and the commentators note the significance, for the withdrawal is the preparation, and the scheme is the gathering, and the verse is the record of the withdrawal, the preparation of the falsehood. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the withdrawal is the preparation, and the scheme is the gathering, and the verse is the record of the withdrawal, the preparation that the following verses describe.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the withdrawal, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the preparation of the falsehood is the thing that the truth awaits, and the one who watches the scheme is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the waiting is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the withdrawal of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the scheme of the falsehood is the prelude of its fall, and the believer who reads the withdrawal is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the waiting: Pharaoh gathered and returned, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the waiting is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the withdrawal is the teaching that the scheme is the prelude of the fall. The verse is the record of the withdrawal, and the teaching of the patience that the waiting requires.",
  "61": "“Moses warned the magicians, “Woe to you! Do not fabricate a lie against Allah, or He will wipe you out with a torment. Whoever fabricates ˹lies˺ is bound to fail.”” records the warning, and the commentators note the two parts of the warning. The verse records the warning: “Woe to you! Do not fabricate a lie against Allah, or He will wipe you out with a torment. Whoever fabricates lies is bound to fail,” or “qala lahum Moosa waylakum la taftaroo ‘ala Allahi kathiban fa-yushitakum bi-‘athabin wa qad khaba mani iftara,” and the commentators note the warning, for Moses warned the magicians before the contest, and the warning is the mercy, the caution of the truth, and the verse is the record of the warning, the mercy that the following verse shows the effect of, and the commentators note the significance, for the warning of Moses is the seed of the conversion, and the verse is the record of the warning, the caution that the magicians heard. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the warning is the mercy, and the caution is the seed, and the verse is the record of the warning, the caution that the following verses fulfill.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own calling. The believer is taught that the warning is the mercy, and the one who warns the wrongdoer is the one whose mercy the Lord values, and the believer who cautions before the deed is the believer whose caution the Lord accepts, and the warning of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the caution is the duty, and the believer who reads the warning is the believer whose mercy the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the warning: Moses warned the magicians, and the believer who cautions the wrongdoer is the believer whose mercy the Lord values, and the warning is the teaching that the caution is the duty. The verse is the record of the warning, and the teaching of the mercy that the warning displays.",
  "62": "“So the magicians disputed the matter among themselves, conversing privately.”” records the dispute, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the dispute: “So the magicians disputed the matter among themselves, conversing privately,” or “fa-tanaza‘oo amrahum baynahum wa asarroo an-najwa,” and the commentators note the dispute, for the magicians disputed the matter, and the verse is the record of the dispute, the effect of the warning, and the commentators note the significance, for the dispute is the doubt, and the verse is the record of the dispute, the first stirring of the conversion. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the dispute is the doubt, and the doubt is the seed, and the verse is the record of the dispute, the effect that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the dispute, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the warning of the truth is the thing that stirs the falsehood, and the one who disputes is the one whose doubt the truth has planted, and the believer who delivers the warning is the believer whose warning the Lord uses, and the dispute of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the warning is the seed, and the believer who reads the dispute is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the effect of the warning: the magicians disputed, and the believer who delivers the warning is the believer whose warning the Lord uses, and the dispute is the teaching that the warning is the seed of the doubt. The verse is the record of the dispute, and the teaching of the certainty that the warning produces.",
  "63": "“They concluded, “These two are only magicians who want to drive you out of your land with their magic, and do away with your most cherished traditions.”” records the conclusion, and the commentators note the two parts of the conclusion. The verse records the conclusion: “These two are only magicians who want to drive you out of your land with their magic, and do away with your most cherished traditions,” or “qaloo in hathani la-sahirani yureedani an yukhrijakum min ardikum bi-sihrihima wa yathhaba bi-tareeqatikumu al-muthla,” and the commentators note the conclusion, for the magicians concluded the accusation, and the conclusion is the label, the repetition of the charge, and the verse is the record of the conclusion, the accusation that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the conclusion is the persuasion, and the verse is the record of the conclusion, the accusation of the falsehood. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the conclusion is the label, and the persuasion is the aim, and the verse is the record of the conclusion, the accusation that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the conclusion, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the falsehood labels the truth, and the one who concludes the accusation is the one whose label the truth exposes, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the conclusion of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the label is the refuge of the falsehood, and the believer who reads the conclusion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the label: the magicians concluded the accusation, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the conclusion is the teaching that the label is the thing that the truth exposes. The verse is the record of the conclusion, and the teaching of the certainty that the label cannot shake.",
  "64": "““So orchestrate your plan, then come forward in ˹perfect˺ ranks. And whoever prevails today will certainly be successful.”” records the counsel, and the commentators note the two parts of the counsel. The verse records the counsel: “So orchestrate your plan, then come forward in perfect ranks. And whoever prevails today will certainly be successful,” or “fa-ajmi‘oo kaydakum thumma i’too saffan wa qad aflaha al-yawma mani ista‘la,” and the commentators note the counsel, for the magicians counselled the gathering and the ranks, and the counsel is the confidence, the preparation of the contest, and the verse is the record of the counsel, the confidence that the following verse opens the contest with, and the commentators note the significance, for the counsel is the confidence of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the counsel, the preparation of the magicians. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the counsel is the confidence, and the ranks are the preparation, and the verse is the record of the counsel, the confidence that the following verses display.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the counsel, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own confidence. The believer is taught that the confidence of the falsehood is the prelude, and the one who watches the preparation is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the counsel of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the confidence is the prelude of the fall, and the believer who reads the counsel is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prelude of the fall: the magicians counselled and lost, and the believer who trusts the truth is the believer whose confidence the Lord confirms, and the counsel is the teaching that the confidence of the falsehood is the prelude of its fall. The verse is the record of the counsel, and the teaching of the confidence that the truth supplies.",
  "65": "“They said, “O Moses! Either you cast, or let us be the first to cast.”” records the offer, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the offer: “O Moses! Either you cast, or let us be the first to cast,” or “qaloo ya Moosa imma an tulqiya wa imma an nakoona awwala man alqa,” and the commentators note the offer, for the magicians offered the choice, and the offer is the courtesy, the confidence of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the offer, the choice that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the offer is the confidence, and the verse is the record of the offer, the choice of the contest. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the offer is the courtesy, and the choice is the contest, and the verse is the record of the offer, the choice that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the offer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own confidence. The believer is taught that the courtesy of the falsehood is the confidence, and the one who is offered the choice is the one whose truth the Lord displays, and the believer who faces the falsehood is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the offer of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the falsehood is the confident, and the believer who reads the offer is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the confidence of the falsehood: the magicians offered the choice, and the believer who faces the falsehood is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the offer is the teaching that the confidence is the prelude of the fall. The verse is the record of the offer, and the teaching of the confidence that the truth supplies.",
  "66": "“Moses responded, “No, you go first.” And suddenly their ropes and staffs appeared to him—by their magic—to be slithering.”” records the reply and the display, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the reply: “No, you go first. And suddenly their ropes and staffs appeared to him—by their magic—to be slithering,” or “qala bal alqoo fa-itha hibalukum wa ‘isiyyukum yukhayyalu ilayhi min sihrihim annaha tas‘a,” and the commentators note the reply, for Moses commanded the magicians to cast first, and the display is the magic, the ropes and the staffs that appeared to slither, and the verse is the record of the reply and the display, the opening of the contest, and the commentators note the significance, for the casting of the falsehood first is the strategy, and the display is the illusion, and the verse is the record of the reply, the opening that the following verse describes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the strategy, and the display is the illusion, and the verse is the record of the reply, the opening of the contest.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the strategy, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own confidence. The believer is taught that the truth lets the falsehood show its full, and the one who commands the casting is the one whose confidence the Lord values, and the believer who faces the display is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the reply of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the truth does not fear the display, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the strategy of the truth: Moses let the magicians cast, and the believer who faces the falsehood is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the truth shows itself after the falsehood. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the confidence that the strategy requires.",
  "67": "“So Moses concealed fear within himself.”” records the fear, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the fear: “So Moses concealed fear within himself,” or “fa-awjasa fee nafsihi kheefatan Moosa,” and the commentators note the fear, for Moses felt the fear at the display, and the verse is the record of the fear, the human reaction, and the commentators note the significance, for the fear of Moses is the human, and the verse is the record of the fear, the humanity that the following verse calms. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the fear is the human, and the concealment is the restraint, and the verse is the record of the fear, the humanity that the following verse reassures.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fear, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own humanity. The believer is taught that the fear of the trial is the human, and the one who feels the fear is the one whose fear the Lord calms, and the believer who trusts the Lord in the fear is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the fear of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the fear is the human, and the believer who reads the fear is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humanity of the fear: Moses feared and was reassured, and the believer who feels the fear is the believer whose fear the Lord calms, and the fear is the teaching that the trust is the remedy. The verse is the record of the fear, and the teaching of the trust that the reassurance produces.",
  "68": "“We reassured ˹him˺, “Do not fear! It is certainly you who will prevail.”” records the reassurance, and the commentators note the two parts of the reassurance. The verse records the reassurance: “Do not fear! It is certainly you who will prevail,” or “qulna la takhaf innaka anta al-a‘la,” and the commentators note the reassurance, for the Lord calmed the fear and promised the victory, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the reassurance is the timing, the arrival at the moment of the fear, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reassurance is the comfort, and the promise is the victory, and the verse is the record of the reassurance, the comfort that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reassurance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the reassurance of the Lord is the comfort, and the one who fears is the one whom the Lord calms, and the believer who trusts the promise is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the reassurance to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the victory is the certain, and the believer who reads the reassurance is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the comfort of the Lord: Moses was reassured and prevailed, and the believer who trusts the promise is the believer whose fear the Lord calms, and the reassurance is the teaching that the victory is the certain. The verse is the record of the reassurance, and the teaching of the trust that the promise produces.",
  "69": "““Cast what is in your right hand, and it will swallow up what they have made, for what they have made is no more than a magic trick. And magicians can never succeed wherever they go.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: “Cast what is in your right hand, and it will swallow up what they have made, for what they have made is no more than a magic trick. And magicians can never succeed wherever they go,” or “wa alqi ma fee yameenika talqaf ma sana‘oo innama sana‘oo kaydu sahirin wa la yuflihu as-sahiru haythu ata,” and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded the casting, and the verse is the record of the command, the instruction that the following verse fulfills, and the commentators note the significance, for the swallowing is the judgment, and the failure of the magician is the law, and the verse is the record of the command, the instruction of the victory. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the casting is the command, and the swallowing is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the command, the instruction that the following verse fulfills.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the truth swallows the falsehood, and the one who casts the truth is the one whose victory the Lord grants, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the command to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the falsehood is the thing that the truth devours, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the judgment of the truth: the staff swallowed the magic, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the command is the teaching that the falsehood is the thing that the truth devours. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the certainty that the power produces.",
  "70": "“So the magicians fell down in prostration, declaring, “We believe in the Lord of Aaron and Moses.”” records the prostration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the prostration: “So the magicians fell down in prostration, declaring, ‘We believe in the Lord of Aaron and Moses,’” or “fa-ulqiya as-saharatu sujjadan qaloo amanna bi-rabbi Haroona wa Moosa,” and the commentators note the prostration, for the magicians fell and declared the faith, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the conversion that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the prostration is the conversion, and the declaration is the confession, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the conversion of the experts. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the prostration is the conversion, and the declaration is the confession, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the conversion that the following verses develop.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the conversion, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the truth converts the sincere, and the one who sees the proof is the one whose conversion the Lord grants, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the prostration of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the experts confess, and the believer who reads the prostration is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the truth: the magicians prostrated, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the prostration is the teaching that the truth converts the sincere. The verse is the record of the prostration, and the teaching of the certainty that the truth produces.",
  "71": "“Pharaoh threatened, “How dare you believe in him before I give you permission? He must be your master who taught you magic. I will certainly cut off your hands and feet on opposite sides, and crucify you on the trunks of palm trees. You will really see whose punishment is more severe and more lasting.”” records the threat, and the commentators note the two parts of the threat. The verse records the threat: “How dare you believe in him before I give you permission?… I will certainly cut off your hands and feet on opposite sides, and crucify you on the trunks of palm trees,” or “qala amantum lahu qabla an athana lakum… la-uqatti‘anna aydiyakum wa arjulakum min khilafin wa la-usallibannakum fee juthoo‘i an-nakhli,” and the commentators note the threat, for Pharaoh threatened the amputation and the crucifixion, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the threat is the violence of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence that the faith of the magicians would withstand. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the threat is the violence, and the amputation is the punishment, and the verse is the record of the threat, the violence that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the threat, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own steadfastness. The believer is taught that the threat of the falsehood is the trial, and the one who is threatened is the one whose faith the Lord tests, and the believer who holds the faith in the face of the threat is the believer whose steadfastness the Lord values, and the threat of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the violence is the thing that the faith withstands, and the believer who reads the threat is the believer whose steadfastness the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trial of the threat: the magicians were threatened and held, and the believer who holds the faith is the believer whose steadfastness the Lord values, and the threat is the teaching that the violence is the test. The verse is the record of the threat, and the teaching of the steadfastness that the threat demands.",
  "72": "“They responded, “By the One Who created us! We will never prefer you over the clear proofs that have come to us. So do whatever you want! Your authority only covers the ˹fleeting˺ life of this world.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “By the One Who created us! We will never prefer you over the clear proofs that have come to us. So do whatever you want! Your authority only covers the fleeting life of this world,” or “qaloo lan nu’thiraka ‘ala ma ja’ana mina al-bayyinati wallathee fatarana faqdi ma anta qadin innama taqdee hathihi al-hayata ad-dunya,” and the commentators note the reply, for the magicians declared the preference of the truth, and the reply is the faith, the steadfastness of the converted, and the verse is the record of the reply, the faith that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the oath is the faith, and the preference is the certainty, and the verse is the record of the reply, the faith of the magicians. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the oath is the faith, and the preference is the certainty, and the verse is the record of the reply, the faith that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the faith, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own steadfastness. The believer is taught that the faith is the preference of the truth, and the one who prefers the truth is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the certainty is the believer whose steadfastness the Lord confirms, and the reply of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the believer prefers, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the preference of the truth: the magicians preferred the truth, and the believer who holds the certainty is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the truth is the preference. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the faith that the preference requires.",
  "73": "““Indeed, we have believed in our Lord so He may forgive our sins and that magic you have forced us to practice. And Allah is far superior ˹in reward˺ and more lasting ˹in punishment˺.”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the reply: “Indeed, we have believed in our Lord so He may forgive our sins and that magic you have forced us to practice. And Allah is far superior in reward and more lasting in punishment,” or “inna amanna bi-rabbina li-yaghfira lana khatayana wa ma akrahtana ‘alayhi mina as-sihri wallahu khayrun wa abqa,” and the commentators note the completion, for the magicians declared the hope of the forgiveness, and the verse is the record of the completion, the faith and the hope, and the commentators note the significance, for the hope of the forgiveness is the faith, and the superiority of the Lord is the certainty, and the verse is the record of the completion, the faith of the converted. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the hope is the faith, and the superiority is the certainty, and the verse is the record of the completion, the faith that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the hope, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own repentance. The believer is taught that the hope of the forgiveness is the faith, and the one who repents is the one whose forgiveness the Lord grants, and the believer who hopes the mercy is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the completion of the magicians is the teaching, the declaration that the repentance is the path of the forgiveness, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the hope of the repentance: the magicians hoped the forgiveness, and the believer who repents is the believer whose forgiveness the Lord grants, and the completion is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the believer seeks. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the hope that the repentance carries.",
  "74": "“Whoever comes to their Lord as an evildoer will certainly have Hell, where they can neither live nor die.”” records the first destiny, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the destiny: “Whoever comes to their Lord as an evildoer will certainly have Hell, where they can neither live nor die,” or “innahu man ya’ti rabbahu mujriman fa-inna lahu jahannama la yamootu feeha wa la yahya,” and the commentators note the destiny, for the evildoer is the one whose end is the Hell, and the description of the neither living nor dying is the torment, and the verse is the record of the destiny, the warning that the following verse contrasts, and the commentators note the significance, for the two destinies are the two ends, and the verse is the record of the destiny, the warning of the Fire. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the evildoer is the end, and the neither living nor dying is the torment, and the verse is the record of the destiny, the warning that the following verse contrasts.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the destiny, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the end of the evildoer is the Fire, and the one who wrongs is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who fears the end is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the destiny of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the two ends are the two ways, and the believer who reads the destiny is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the wrong: the evildoer is the Fire, and the believer who fears the end is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the destiny is the teaching that the wrong is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the destiny, and the teaching of the fear that the end produces.",
  "75": "“But whoever comes to Him as a believer, having done good, they will have the highest ranks:” records the second destiny, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the destiny: “But whoever comes to Him as a believer, having done good, they will have the highest ranks,” or “wa man ya’tihi mu’minan qad ‘amila as-salihati fa-ula’ika lahumu ad-darajatu al-‘ula,” and the commentators note the destiny, for the believer is the one whose end is the highest ranks, and the verse is the record of the destiny, the promise that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the two conditions are the faith and the good, and the highest ranks are the reward, and the verse is the record of the destiny, the promise of the Paradise. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the faith and the good are the conditions, and the ranks are the reward, and the verse is the record of the destiny, the promise that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the destiny, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the end of the believer is the highest ranks, and the one who believes and does the good is the one whose end the Lord raises, and the believer who hopes the ranks is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the destiny of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the faith and the good are the path of the elevation, and the believer who reads the destiny is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the good: the believer is the ranks, and the believer who hopes the elevation is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the destiny is the teaching that the faith and the good are the path. The verse is the record of the destiny, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "76": "“the Gardens of Eternity, under which rivers flow, where they will stay forever. That is the reward of those who purify themselves.”” describes the reward, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse describes the reward: “the Gardens of Eternity, under which rivers flow, where they will stay forever. That is the reward of those who purify themselves,” or “jannatu ‘adnin tajree min tahtiha al-anharu khalideena feeha wa thalika jaza’u man tazakka,” and the commentators note the description, for the Gardens are the eternal, and the reward is for the purified, and the verse is the record of the description, the reward that the following verse applies, and the commentators note the significance, for the eternity is the permanence, and the purification is the condition, and the verse is the record of the description, the reward of the taqwa. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the Gardens are the eternal, and the purification is the condition, and the verse is the record of the description, the reward that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reward, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own purification. The believer is taught that the reward of the Lord is for the purified, and the one who purifies the self is the one whose reward the Lord grants, and the believer who lives the purity is the believer whose reward the Lord confirms, and the description of the Gardens is the teaching, the declaration that the purification is the path of the reward, and the believer who reads the description is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the purity: the Gardens are for the purified, and the believer who purifies the self is the believer whose reward the Lord grants, and the description is the teaching that the purity is the path. The verse is the record of the description, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "77": "“And We surely inspired Moses, ˹saying,˺ “Leave with My servants ˹at night˺ and strike a dry passage for them across the sea. Have no fear of being overtaken, nor be concerned ˹of drowning˺.”” records the command of the exodus, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: “Leave with My servants at night and strike a dry passage for them across the sea. Have no fear of being overtaken, nor be concerned of drowning,” or “wa laqad awhayna ila Moosa an asri bi-‘ibadee fadrib lahum tareeqan fi al-bahri yabasan la takhafu darakan wa la takhsha,” and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded the night departure and the striking of the sea, and the verse is the record of the command, the instruction of the exodus, and the commentators note the significance, for the night is the departure, and the striking is the miracle, and the verse is the record of the command, the instruction that the following verse fulfills. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the instruction, and the striking is the miracle, and the verse is the record of the command, the instruction of the exodus.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the command of the Lord is the guidance, and the one who follows the command is the one whose affair the Lord arranges, and the believer who trusts the guidance is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the command to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the trust is the path of the safety, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the guidance of the exodus: Moses was commanded and obeyed, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose affair the Lord arranges, and the command is the teaching that the trust is the safety. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the trust that the guidance produces.",
  "78": "“Then Pharaoh pursued them with his soldiers—but how overwhelming were the waters that submerged them!”” records the drowning, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the drowning: “Then Pharaoh pursued them with his soldiers—but how overwhelming were the waters that submerged them!” or “fa-atba‘ahum Fir‘awnu bi-junoodihi fa-ghashiyahum mina al-yammi ma ghashiyahum,” and the commentators note the drowning, for Pharaoh pursued and the waters submerged, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the pursuit is the aggression, and the drowning is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment of the sea. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the pursuit is the aggression, and the drowning is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the drowning, the judgment that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the judgment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the judgment of the Lord is the certain, and the one who oppresses is the one whom the Lord drowns, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the drowning of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the tyrant's end is the ruin, and the believer who reads the drowning is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the tyrant: Pharaoh drowned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the drowning is the teaching that the oppression ends in the ruin. The verse is the record of the drowning, and the teaching of the trust that the judgment produces.",
  "79": "“And ˹so˺ Pharaoh led his people astray, and did not guide ˹them rightly˺.”” records the misguidance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the misguidance: “And so Pharaoh led his people astray, and did not guide them rightly,” or “wa adalla Fir‘awnu qawmahu wa ma hada,” and the commentators note the misguidance, for Pharaoh led the people astray, and the verse is the record of the misguidance, the verdict upon the tyrant, and the commentators note the significance, for the misguidance is the sin, and the absence of the guidance is the verdict, and the verse is the record of the misguidance, the verdict that the following verse addresses. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the misguidance is the sin, and the absence of the guidance is the verdict, and the verse is the record of the misguidance, the verdict upon the tyrant.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the leadership, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own responsibility. The believer is taught that the leadership is the trust, and the one who leads astray is the one whose leadership the Lord condemns, and the believer who leads to the truth is the believer whose leadership the Lord values, and the misguidance of Pharaoh is the teaching, the declaration that the leader is the responsible, and the believer who reads the misguidance is the believer whose responsibility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the leadership: Pharaoh led astray, and the believer who leads to the truth is the believer whose leadership the Lord values, and the misguidance is the teaching that the leader is the responsible. The verse is the record of the misguidance, and the teaching of the responsibility that the leadership carries.",
  "80": "“O Children of Israel! We saved you from your enemy, and made an appointment with you on the right side of Mount Ṭûr, and sent down to you manna and quails,” records the favours, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the favours: “O Children of Israel! We saved you from your enemy, and made an appointment with you on the right side of Mount Tur, and sent down to you manna and quails,” or “ya Banee Isra’eela qad anjaynakum min ‘aduwwikum wa wa‘adnakum janiba at-toori al-aymana wa nazzalna ‘alaykumu al-manna was-salwa,” and the commentators note the favours, for the Lord reminded the people of the three favours, and the verse is the record of the favours, the reminder that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the three favours are the deliverance and the appointment and the provision, and the verse is the record of the favours, the reminder of the grace. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the favours are the three, and the reminder is the gratitude, and the verse is the record of the favours, the reminder that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the favours, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the remembrance of the favours is the gratitude, and the one who recalls the grace is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who remembers the mercies is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the favours of the verse are the teaching, the declaration that the grace is the many, and the believer who reads the favours is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gratitude of the favours: the three were recalled, and the believer who remembers the grace is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the favours are the teaching that the grace is the thing that the believer must recall. The verse is the record of the favours, and the teaching of the gratitude that the grace requires.",
  "81": "“˹saying,˺ “Eat from the good things We have provided for you, but do not transgress in them, or My wrath will befall you. And whoever My wrath befalls is certainly doomed.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: “Eat from the good things We have provided for you, but do not transgress in them, or My wrath will befall you. And whoever My wrath befalls is certainly doomed,” or “kuloo min tayyibati ma razaqnakum wa la tatghaw feehi fa-yahilla ‘alaykum ghadabee wa man yahlil ‘alayhi ghadabee fa-qad hawa,” and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded the eating and forbade the transgression, and the verse is the record of the command, the warning that the following verse balances, and the commentators note the significance, for the transgression is the sin, and the wrath is the warning, and the verse is the record of the command, the warning of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the provision, and the warning is the wrath, and the verse is the record of the command, the warning that the following verse balances.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the transgression is the thing that the wrath answers, and the one who exceeds the limit is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who fears the wrath is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the warning of the Lord is the teaching, the declaration that the transgression is the path of the ruin, and the believer who reads the warning is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the warning of the transgression: the wrath is the warning, and the believer who fears the wrath is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the transgression is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the fear that the warning produces.",
  "82": "“But I am truly Most Forgiving to whoever repents, believes, and does good, then persists on ˹true˺ guidance.”” records the promise, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the promise: “But I am truly Most Forgiving to whoever repents, believes, and does good, then persists on true guidance,” or “wa innee la-ghaffarun liman taba wa amana wa ‘amila salihan thumma ihtada,” and the commentators note the promise, for the Lord declared the forgiveness, and the verse is the record of the promise, the balance that the previous warning required, and the commentators note the significance, for the three conditions are the repentance and the faith and the good, and the persistence is the guidance, and the verse is the record of the promise, the mercy that the warning balanced. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the conditions are the three, and the forgiveness is the promise, and the verse is the record of the promise, the mercy that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the promise, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the forgiveness is for the repentant, and the one who returns is the one whom the Lord forgives, and the believer who hopes the mercy is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the promise of the Lord is the teaching, the declaration that the repentance is the path of the forgiveness, and the believer who reads the promise is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the Lord: the forgiveness is the promise, and the believer who repents is the believer whose forgiveness the Lord grants, and the promise is the teaching that the mercy is the thing that the believer seeks. The verse is the record of the promise, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "83": "“˹Allah asked,˺ “Why have you come with such haste ahead of your people, O Moses?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the question: “Why have you come with such haste ahead of your people, O Moses?” or “wa ma a‘jalaka ‘an qawmika ya Moosa,” and the commentators note the question, for the Lord asked Moses of the haste, and the question is the introduction, the opening of the account of the calf, and the verse is the record of the question, the step that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the occasion, and the verse is the record of the question, the opening of the account. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the occasion, and the haste is the state, and the verse is the record of the question, the opening that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own sincerity. The believer is taught that the question of the Lord is the occasion, and the one who is asked is the one whose answer the Lord values, and the believer who answers with the sincerity is the believer whose sincerity the Lord accepts, and the question to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the sincerity is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose sincerity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the occasion of the question: Moses was asked and answered, and the believer who answers with the sincerity is the believer whose sincerity the Lord values, and the question is the teaching that the sincerity is the answer. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the sincerity that the answer requires.",
  "84": "“He replied, “They are close on my tracks. And I have hastened to You, my Lord, so You will be pleased.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “They are close on my tracks. And I have hastened to You, my Lord, so You will be pleased,” or “qala hum ula’i ‘ala atharee wa ‘ajiltu ilayka rabbi li-tarda,” and the commentators note the reply, for Moses declared the nearness of the people and the haste for the pleasure, and the reply is the sincerity, the declaration of the motive, and the verse is the record of the reply, the sincerity that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the haste for the pleasure is the love, and the verse is the record of the reply, the sincerity of the prophet. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the nearness is the state, and the haste is the love, and the verse is the record of the reply, the sincerity that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the sincerity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own love. The believer is taught that the haste for the pleasure is the love, and the one who seeks the pleasure is the one whose love the Lord values, and the believer who hastens to the Lord is the believer whose haste the Lord accepts, and the reply of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the pleasure is the motive, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose love the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the haste of the love: Moses hastened for the pleasure, and the believer who hastens to the Lord is the believer whose love the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the pleasure is the motive. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the love that the haste displays.",
  "85": "“Allah responded, “We have indeed tested your people in your absence, and the Sâmiri has led them astray.”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The verse records the answer: “We have indeed tested your people in your absence, and the Samiri has led them astray,” or “qala fa-inna qad fatanna qawmaka min ba‘dika wa adallahumu as-Samiriyyu,” and the commentators note the answer, for the Lord declared the test and the misguidance, and the answer is the revelation, the declaration of the calf, and the verse is the record of the answer, the revelation that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the test is the trial, and the Samiri is the instigator, and the verse is the record of the answer, the revelation of the trial. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the test is the trial, and the Samiri is the instigator, and the verse is the record of the answer, the revelation that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the trial, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own vigilance. The believer is taught that the absence is the test, and the one who is tested is the one whose faith the Lord weighs, and the believer who holds the faith in the absence is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the answer to Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the trial is the thing that the believer must expect, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose vigilance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the test of the absence: the people were tested and fell, and the believer who holds the faith in the absence is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the answer is the teaching that the trial is the thing that the believer must guard against. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the vigilance that the trial requires.",
  "86": "“So Moses returned to his people, furious and sorrowful. He said, “O my people! Had your Lord not made you a good promise? Has my absence been too long for you? Or have you wished for wrath from your Lord to befall you, so you broke your promise to me?”” records the return, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the return: “So Moses returned to his people, furious and sorrowful. He said, ‘O my people! Had your Lord not made you a good promise?…’” or “fa-raja‘a Moosa ila qawmihi ghadbana asifan qala ya qawmi alam ya‘idkum rabbukum wa‘dan hasanan afa-tala ‘alaykumu al-‘ahdu,” and the commentators note the return, for Moses returned in the anger and the sorrow, and the rebuke is the question, the reminder of the promise, and the verse is the record of the return, the rebuke that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the anger and the sorrow are the two emotions of the betrayed prophet, and the verse is the record of the return, the rebuke of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the return is the anger, and the rebuke is the question, and the verse is the record of the return, the rebuke that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rebuke, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own constancy. The believer is taught that the break of the promise is the thing that the rebuke answers, and the one who breaks the covenant is the one whose break the Lord judges, and the believer who keeps the promise is the believer whose constancy the Lord values, and the rebuke of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the promise is the trust, and the believer who reads the rebuke is the believer whose constancy the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the promise: Moses rebuked the break, and the believer who keeps the covenant is the believer whose constancy the Lord values, and the rebuke is the teaching that the promise is the trust. The verse is the record of the return, and the teaching of the constancy that the promise requires.",
  "87": "“They argued, “We did not break your promise of our own free will, but we were made to carry the burden of the people’s ˹golden˺ jewellery, then we threw it ˹into the fire˺, and so did the Sâmiri.”” records the excuse, and the commentators note the two parts of the excuse. The verse records the excuse: “We did not break your promise of our own free will, but we were made to carry the burden of the people’s golden jewellery, then we threw it into the fire, and so did the Samiri,” or “qaloo ma akhlafna maw‘idaka bi-malkina wa lakinna hummilna awzaran min zeenati al-qawmi fa-qathafnaha fa-kathalika alqa as-Samiriyyu,” and the commentators note the excuse, for the people blamed the burden and the Samiri, and the excuse is the deflection, the shifting of the fault, and the verse is the record of the excuse, the deflection that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the excuse is the shifting, and the verse is the record of the excuse, the deflection of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the excuse is the shifting, and the deflection is the fault, and the verse is the record of the excuse, the deflection that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the excuse, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own honesty. The believer is taught that the shifting of the fault is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the one who blames the other is the one whose blame the Lord weighs, and the believer who owns the fault is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the excuse of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the shifting is the corruption, and the believer who reads the excuse is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the shifting: the people blamed the Samiri, and the believer who owns the fault is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the excuse is the teaching that the shifting is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the excuse, and the teaching of the honesty that the fault requires.",
  "88": "“Then he moulded for them an idol of a calf that made a lowing sound. They said, “This is your god and the god of Moses, but Moses forgot ˹where it was˺!”” records the calf, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the calf: “Then he moulded for them an idol of a calf that made a lowing sound. They said, ‘This is your god and the god of Moses, but Moses forgot where it was!’” or “fa-akhraja lahum ‘ijlan jasadan lahu khuwarun fa-qaloo hatha ilahukum wa ilahu Moosa fa-nasiya,” and the commentators note the calf, for the Samiri moulded the calf, and the claim of the people, and the verse is the record of the calf, the idol that the following verse refutes, and the commentators note the significance, for the calf is the idol, and the claim is the lie, and the verse is the record of the calf, the idol that the following verse exposes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the calf is the idol, and the lowing is the sound, and the verse is the record of the calf, the idol that the following verse refutes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the idol, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the idol is the thing that the truth refutes, and the one who worships the false is the one whose falsehood the Lord exposes, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the calf of the Samiri is the teaching, the declaration that the idol is the false, and the believer who reads the calf is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the falsehood of the idol: the calf was the false, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the calf is the teaching that the idol is the thing that the truth exposes. The verse is the record of the calf, and the teaching of the certainty that the truth produces.",
  "89": "“Did they not see that it did not respond to them, nor could it protect or benefit them?”” records the refutation, and the commentators note the two parts of the refutation. The verse records the refutation: “Did they not see that it did not respond to them, nor could it protect or benefit them?” or “a-fa-la yarawna alla yarji‘u ilayhim qawlan wa la yamliku lahum darran wa la naf‘an,” and the commentators note the refutation, for the verse exposes the calf, and the refutation is the argument, the declaration that the idol cannot respond or harm or benefit, and the verse is the record of the refutation, the argument that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the three negations are the proof, and the verse is the record of the refutation, the argument of the reason. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the three negations are the proof, and the refutation is the argument, and the verse is the record of the refutation, the argument that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the refutation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the refutation of the false is the argument of the truth, and the one who reasons is the one whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the believer who reflects upon the false is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the refutation of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the idol is the powerless, and the believer who reads the refutation is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the truth: the calf was the powerless, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the refutation is the teaching that the false is the thing that the reason exposes. The verse is the record of the refutation, and the teaching of the certainty that the argument produces.",
  "90": "“Aaron had already warned them beforehand, “O my people! You are only being tested by this, for indeed your ˹one true˺ Lord is the Most Compassionate. So follow me and obey my orders.”” records the warning of Aaron, and the commentators note the two parts of the warning. The verse records the warning: “Aaron had already warned them beforehand, ‘O my people! You are only being tested by this, for indeed your one true Lord is the Most Compassionate. So follow me and obey my orders,’” or “wa laqad qala lahum Haroonu min qablu ya qawmi innama futintum bihi wa inna rabbakumu ar-rahmanu fattabi‘oonee wa atee‘oo amree,” and the commentators note the warning, for Aaron had warned the people, and the warning is the vindication, the declaration that Aaron had tried, and the verse is the record of the warning, the vindication that the following verse shows the people rejecting, and the commentators note the significance, for the warning of Aaron is the proof of his innocence, and the verse is the record of the warning, the vindication of the brother. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the warning is the vindication, and the proof is the innocence, and the verse is the record of the warning, the vindication that the following verse displays.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own duty. The believer is taught that the warning is the duty, and the one who warns is the one whose duty the Lord values, and the believer who calls to the truth is the believer whose call the Lord accepts, and the warning of Aaron is the teaching, the declaration that the warning is the thing that the believer must deliver, and the believer who reads the warning is the believer whose duty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the duty of the warning: Aaron warned and was vindicated, and the believer who warns is the believer whose duty the Lord values, and the warning is the teaching that the delivery is the thing that the believer must do. The verse is the record of the warning, and the teaching of the duty that the warning requires.",
  "91": "“They replied, “We will not cease to worship it until Moses returns to us.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “We will not cease to worship it until Moses returns to us,” or “qaloo lan nabraha ‘alayhi ‘akifeena hatta yarji‘a ilayna Moosa,” and the commentators note the reply, for the people declared the persistence, and the reply is the stubbornness, the declaration that they would not cease until the return, and the verse is the record of the reply, the stubbornness that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the persistence is the sin, and the verse is the record of the reply, the stubbornness of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the stubbornness, and the persistence is the sin, and the verse is the record of the reply, the stubbornness that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the stubbornness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own return. The believer is taught that the persistence in the sin is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the one who delays the return is the one whose delay the Lord weighs, and the believer who hastens the repentance is the believer whose haste the Lord values, and the reply of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the persistence is the corruption, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose return the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the persistence: the people persisted and were judged, and the believer who hastens the return is the believer whose haste the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the persistence is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the return that the repentance requires.",
  "92": "“Moses scolded ˹his brother˺, “O Aaron! What prevented you, when you saw them going astray,”” records the scolding, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the scolding: “Moses scolded his brother, ‘O Aaron! What prevented you, when you saw them going astray,’” or “qala ya Haroonu ma mana‘aka ith ra’aytahum dalloo,” and the commentators note the scolding, for Moses scolded Aaron, and the scolding is the rebuke, the question of the inaction, and the verse is the record of the scolding, the rebuke that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the scolding is the anger, and the verse is the record of the scolding, the rebuke of the brother. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the scolding is the rebuke, and the question is the anger, and the verse is the record of the scolding, the rebuke that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rebuke, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own concern. The believer is taught that the rebuke of the family is the concern, and the one who rebukes is the one whose concern the Lord values, and the believer who cares for the faith is the believer whose care the Lord accepts, and the scolding of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the concern is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the scolding is the believer whose concern the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the concern of the rebuke: Moses scolded Aaron, and the believer who cares for the faith is the believer whose concern the Lord values, and the scolding is the teaching that the concern is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the scolding, and the teaching of the concern that the rebuke displays.",
  "93": "“from following after me? How could you disobey my orders?”” completes the scolding, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the scolding: “from following after me? How could you disobey my orders?” or “alla tattabi‘ani a-fa-‘asayta amree,” and the commentators note the completion, for Moses asked the reason of the disobedience, and the completion is the rebuke, the question of the following and the obedience, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rebuke that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the rebuke, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rebuke of the brother. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the rebuke, and the completion is the anger, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rebuke that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the obedience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own duty. The believer is taught that the obedience of the command is the duty, and the one who disobeys is the one whose disobedience the Lord weighs, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the completion of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the trust, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the duty of the obedience: Moses rebuked the disobedience, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the obedience is the trust. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the obedience that the command requires.",
  "94": "“Aaron pleaded, “O son of my mother! Do not seize me by my beard or ˹the hair of˺ my head. I really feared that you would say, ‘You have caused division among the Children of Israel, and did not observe my word.’”” records the plea, and the commentators note the two parts of the plea. The verse records the plea: “O son of my mother! Do not seize me by my beard or the hair of my head. I really feared that you would say, ‘You have caused division among the Children of Israel, and did not observe my word,’” or “qala yabna umma la ta’khuth bi-lihyatee wa la bi-ra’see innee khasheetu an taqoola farraqta bayna Banee Isra’eela wa lam tarqub qawlee,” and the commentators note the plea, for Aaron pleaded the brotherhood and the fear of the division, and the plea is the vindication, the explanation of the inaction, and the verse is the record of the plea, the vindication that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the address \"son of my mother\" is the tenderness, and the fear of the division is the explanation, and the verse is the record of the plea, the vindication of Aaron. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the plea is the vindication, and the fear is the explanation, and the verse is the record of the plea, the vindication that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the plea, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own explanation. The believer is taught that the explanation of the act is the duty, and the one who explains is the one whose explanation the Lord values, and the believer who clarifies the matter is the believer whose clarity the Lord accepts, and the plea of Aaron is the teaching, the declaration that the explanation is the thing that the believer must offer, and the believer who reads the plea is the believer whose clarity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the explanation of the act: Aaron explained and was vindicated, and the believer who clarifies is the believer whose explanation the Lord values, and the plea is the teaching that the explanation is the thing that the believer must offer. The verse is the record of the plea, and the teaching of the clarity that the explanation requires.",
  "95": "“Moses then asked, “What did you think you were doing, O Sâmiri?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the question: “What did you think you were doing, O Samiri?” or “qala fa-ma khatbuka ya Samiriyyu,” and the commentators note the question, for Moses turned to the Samiri, and the question is the examination, the opening of the account of the instigator, and the verse is the record of the question, the step that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the examination, and the verse is the record of the question, the examination of the instigator. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the examination, and the Samiri is the instigator, and the verse is the record of the question, the examination that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own discernment. The believer is taught that the examination of the deed is the duty, and the one who asks is the one whose discernment the Lord values, and the believer who examines the acts is the believer whose discernment the Lord accepts, and the question of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the examination is the thing that the believer must do, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose discernment the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the examination of the deed: Moses asked the Samiri, and the believer who examines the acts is the believer whose discernment the Lord values, and the question is the teaching that the examination is the duty. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the discernment that the examination requires.",
  "96": "“He said, “I saw what they did not see, so I took a handful ˹of dust˺ from the hoof-prints of ˹the horse of˺ the messenger-angel ˹Gabriel˺ then cast it ˹on the moulded calf˺. This is what my lower-self tempted me into.”” records the confession, and the commentators note the two parts of the confession. The verse records the confession: “I saw what they did not see, so I took a handful of dust from the hoof-prints of the horse of the messenger-angel Gabriel then cast it on the moulded calf. This is what my lower-self tempted me into,” or “qala basurtu bima lam yabsuroo bihi fa-qabadtu qabdatan min athari ar-rasooli fa-nabathtuha wa kathalika sawwalat lee nafsee,” and the commentators note the confession, for the Samiri confessed the deed, and the confession is the explanation, the admission of the temptation, and the verse is the record of the confession, the explanation that the following verse answers with the judgment, and the commentators note the significance, for the confession is the admission, and the verse is the record of the confession, the explanation of the instigator. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the confession is the admission, and the temptation is the cause, and the verse is the record of the confession, the explanation that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the confession, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own honesty. The believer is taught that the confession of the fault is the thing that the believer must make, and the one who admits is the one whose honesty the Lord values, and the believer who owns the deed is the believer whose honesty the Lord accepts, and the confession of the Samiri is the teaching, the declaration that the admission is the thing that the believer must offer, and the believer who reads the confession is the believer whose honesty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honesty of the admission: the Samiri confessed, and the believer who owns the fault is the believer whose honesty the Lord values, and the confession is the teaching that the admission is the thing that the believer must make. The verse is the record of the confession, and the teaching of the honesty that the admission requires.",
  "97": "“Moses said, “Go away then! And for ˹the rest of your˺ life you will surely be crying, ‘Do not touch ˹me˺!’ Then you will certainly have a fate that you cannot escape. Now look at your god to which you have been devoted: we will burn it up, then scatter it in the sea completely.”” records the judgment, and the commentators note the two parts of the judgment. The verse records the judgment: “Go away then!… Now look at your god to which you have been devoted: we will burn it up, then scatter it in the sea completely,” or “qala fathhab fa-inna laka fi al-hayati an taqoola la misasa wa inna laka maw‘idan lan tukhlafahu wanzur ila ilahika allathee zalta ‘alayhi ‘akifan la-nuharriqannahu thumma la-nansifannahu fi al-yammi nasfan,” and the commentators note the judgment, for Moses condemned the Samiri and burned the calf, and the judgment is the recompense, the declaration of the exile and the destruction of the idol, and the verse is the record of the judgment, the recompense that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the judgment is the justice, and the burning is the destruction of the false, and the verse is the record of the judgment, the recompense of the instigator. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the judgment is the exile, and the burning is the destruction, and the verse is the record of the judgment, the recompense that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the judgment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own justice. The believer is taught that the judgment of the wrong is the justice, and the one who judges is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who destroys the false is the believer whose justice the Lord accepts, and the judgment of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the wrong is the thing that the believer must judge, and the believer who reads the judgment is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the justice of the judgment: Moses condemned and destroyed, and the believer who judges the wrong is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the judgment is the teaching that the false is the thing that the believer must destroy. The verse is the record of the judgment, and the teaching of the justice that the wrong requires.",
  "98": "“˹Then Moses addressed his people,˺ “Your only god is Allah, there is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him. He encompasses everything in ˹His˺ knowledge.”” closes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse closes the account: “Your only god is Allah, there is no god worthy of worship except Him. He encompasses everything in His knowledge,” or “innama ilahukumu Allahu allathee la ilaha illa huwa wasi‘a kulla shay’in ‘ilman,” and the commentators note the closing, for Moses declared the oneness, and the verse is the record of the closing, the declaration that the following verses develop, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration of the oneness is the answer to the calf, and the encompassing knowledge is the attribute, and the verse is the record of the closing, the declaration of the truth. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the oneness is the truth, and the knowledge is the attribute, and the verse is the record of the closing, the declaration that the following verses develop.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the oneness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own faith. The believer is taught that the oneness is the truth, and the one who declares the oneness is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the oneness is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the closing of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the oneness is the answer to the false, and the believer who reads the closing is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the oneness: Moses declared the One, and the believer who holds the oneness is the believer whose faith the Lord confirms, and the closing is the teaching that the oneness is the thing that the believer must declare. The verse is the record of the closing, and the teaching of the faith that the oneness establishes.",
  "99": "\"This is how We relate to you\" is the Quran's own comment on the story it has just told, and the address has shifted. God is no longer speaking to Moses or to the Children of Israel. He is speaking to the Prophet Muhammad, the final messenger, and through him to every reader, explaining why these ancient stories are being told at all: \"some of the stories of the past.\" The word \"some\" is significant. The Quran does not claim to be a history book. It selects from the past what teaches, what warns, and what heals, and the selection itself is part of the message. The stories of Moses, Pharaoh, the magicians, and the calf are not entertainment. They are mirrors held up to the present.\n\nThe second half of the verse names the source of the stories: \"And We have certainly granted you a Reminder from Us.\" The word for \"Reminder\" is 'dhikr,' and it is one of the Quran's names for itself, because its function is to remind hearts of what they already know deep down and have forgotten. The Quran elsewhere says the same thing about its own storytelling: \"We relate to you ˹O Prophet˺ the best of stories through Our revelation of this Quran, though before this you were totally unaware ˹of them˺.\" The Prophet had never read these histories and had no human teacher, so the fact that he could tell them in such detail was itself proof that the Reminder came from God. The verse also explains the order of the chapter itself. The long story of Moses was not told for its own sake. It was told to comfort the Prophet in Mecca, where he faced a powerful and mocking opposition, by showing that his experience repeated a pattern as old as revelation: the messenger is accused, the tyrant resists, the believers are rescued, and the truth stands when the illusion falls. The Reminder retells the past so that the present can see its own ending in it.",
  "100": "\"Whoever turns away from it\" names the one choice the Reminder always forces, and the verb behind \"turns away\" is the same word used for Pharaoh and for the people of the calf: to turn one's back, to refuse, to walk in the other direction. The Reminder has been granted, the stories have been told, and the door now stands in front of every listener. A person either faces it or turns away, and the verse states the consequence of turning: \"will surely bear the burden ˹of sin˺ on the Day of Judgment\" The word for \"burden\" is 'wizr,' a heavy load carried on the back, and the image is exact: the one who turns away does not escape the Reminder by ignoring it. He picks it up as a weight instead, and the weight is carried to the Day of Judgment.\n\nThe verse also implies the fairness that the Quran states openly elsewhere: \"No soul burdened with sin will bear the burden of another.\" The burden here is personal. No one carries it for anyone else, and no one is crushed by a weight they did not choose. The sentence is the first half of a picture that the next verse completes, where the burden becomes a permanent load from which there is no laying down. For the reader moving through these verses, the logic is clear and patient: the story has been told, the Reminder has been granted, and now each person is shown the two directions and their destinations. Turning toward it is life; turning away is a weight that no one can carry forever, and the Day of Judgment is when every back will feel what it chose to carry. The image of the burden also returns to the people of the calf. They carried the weight of their jewelry into sin, and the verse closes the story by describing the weight that sin itself becomes. A burden carried willingly in this world, the verse warns, will be felt on the Day of Judgment, when every carrier discovers what their load actually weighs.",
  "101": "\"suffering its consequences forever\" completes the sentence the previous verse began, and the completion is the heavy part. The person who turns away from the Reminder does not merely carry a burden on the Day of Judgment; the verse now says that the carrying is permanent. The Arabic word behind \"forever\" is the word of endless abiding, the same word the Quran uses for the eternal stay of the people of Paradise. What changes is the content of the stay. Some abide in gardens, and some abide under a burden, and the one who ignored the Reminder has chosen which kind of abiding will be his.\n\nThen the verse turns to the reader with an exclamation: \"What an evil burden they will carry on Judgment Day!\" The word for \"burden\" is 'himl,' the load carried on the back, and the word \"evil\" is the Quran's strongest way of condemning something: how evil is this load, how terrible is the thing they will be made to carry. The verse is not a dry statement of law. It is a cry of pity and warning at once, the way a witness to a disaster cries out as it happens.\n\nTogether, verses 100 and 101 form one complete picture: the burden is carried, the carrying never ends, and the load is evil beyond description. The chapter placed this picture right after the story of the calf, and the connection is deliberate. The calf-worshippers carried their sin in the desert, repented, and were forgiven, because the door described earlier, \"But I am truly Most Forgiving\", was still open to them. The burden of the Day of Judgment is what remains for the one who never walks through that door. The story of the calf shows the door; these verses describe the weight on the other side of refusing it.",
  "102": "\"the Day the Trumpet will be blown\" moves the chapter from the burden of the individual to the scene of the whole creation, and it opens with a warning: beware of this Day. The word for \"Trumpet\" is 'as-sur,' the horn whose blowing the Quran uses again and again for the end of the world and the beginning of the resurrection. The image is simple and enormous: a single sound, and everything changes. The dead rise, the graves empty, and every human being who ever lived is gathered in one place. The verse does not describe the mechanics of the blowing; it describes what the gathered will look like: \"We will gather the wicked on that Day blue-faced ˹from horror and thirst˺.\" The word behind \"blue-faced\" is 'zurqan,' a word used for the discoloring of the face by terror and thirst, and the commentators explain it as the look of a person whose fear has drained the life from their face.\n\nThe wicked are singled out here because the scene is theirs to fear. They are gathered, and their faces already announce what they were. The verse is the Quran's way of making the Last Day vivid: not an idea, but a morning on which faces will be the first witnesses, showing the horror inside before a word is spoken. The trumpet, the gathering, the faces: three strokes, and the reader is standing inside the scene. The warning that opens the verse, \"Beware of\", is worth pausing on as well. The Quran does not describe this Day as distant folklore. It addresses the living directly, because the only person the Day can still help is the one who hears about it before it comes. After it comes, the faces will tell the story on their own. What the gathered will say to one another comes next, and their words are the saddest part of the picture.",
  "103": "\"They will whisper among themselves\" describes the sound of the gathered wicked on that Day, and the verb is precise. The Arabic is 'yatakhafatuna,' a mutual, hushed whispering, the sound of people speaking under the pressure of an overwhelming place. They do not shout, and they do not preach. They whisper, and the content of the whisper is their first realization: \"You stayed no more than ten days ˹on the earth˺.\" The whole of earthly life, the decades of plans, houses, rivalries, and pleasures, shrinks in their memory to ten days. One of them guesses the length of the life they lived, and the guess is small.\n\nThe verse is the Quran's most compact teaching about the brevity of life. It does not argue that life is short. It places the reader on the far side of death and lets the dead themselves say it, and their estimate is the one that matters. The same idea appears across the Quran in different words: on the Day they are gathered, \"it will be as if they had not stayed ˹in the world˺ except for an hour of a day\". From inside life, a lifetime feels long. From the other side, it feels like ten days, or one day, or an hour. The difference between the two estimates is the difference between the person who wastes the world and the person who invests it, and the verse leaves the reader holding both measurements at once. There is also a difference between the two guesses the verse reports. Ten days is the guess of the crowd, and one day is the guess of the wisest among them. The Quran is not measuring the length of life but the honesty of hindsight. The more sound a person's judgement, the smaller the world becomes in their memory, because sound judgement measures everything against its true scale.",
  "104": "\"We know best what they will say\" is God's comment on the whispers, and it does two things at once. It assures the listener that nothing spoken on that Day is hidden, and it prepares for a correction: the estimates the people will trade are all wrong, and the verse now gives the most accurate one they will manage: \"the most reasonable of them will say\", and the most reasonable will say \"You stayed no more than a day\". The phrase \"most reasonable\" translates 'amthaluhum tariqatan,' the most sound of them in judgment, the wisest among them. Even the best minds of the gathering, the ones whose thinking was most reliable in the world, will reduce the whole of earthly life to a single day. The verse is not recording a dispute about chronology. It is recording the collapse of every measurement a human being makes from inside the world.\n\nThere is also a quiet irony in the scene. These are the same people who, in the world, treated the Day of Judgment as remote and improbable, who asked when it would come as if it could not be near. Now that it has come, the remote thing turns out to be their own lives. The Day they mocked as far away is standing on top of them, and the life they treated as endless is remembered as one day. God's knowledge surrounds the whisper and the whisperers, and the verse places the reader in that knowledge, hearing what they will say before they say it. The point is not their arithmetic. It is the reversal the Day brings, when the long becomes short and the dismissed becomes present. The verse is also a mercy in its timing. God records the correction now, in the world, while the scale can still be used, so that the people who hear it do not have to wait for the Day to learn what the Day will teach.",
  "105": "\"My Lord will wipe them out completely\" is the answer God gives His Prophet to a question about the mountains, and the question came from the disbelievers of Mecca. They asked what would become of the mountains on the Day the Prophet kept warning about, and the question was partly mockery: could the God of this messenger really level the mountains that stood over their valleys? The answer does not negotiate. \"My Lord will wipe them out completely\" uses the word 'nasf,' which means to crush and pulverize, the way sand is blown away, and the word \"completely\" closes the matter: nothing will remain. The mountains that looked eternal to the questioners are, in God's plan, a handful of dust.\n\nThe verse teaches something about the scale of the Day of Judgment. Human beings measure power by mountains, empires, and monuments, and the Quran answers by removing the measure. On that Day the solid world itself will be dismantled, because the Day is not a continuation of this world but its replacement. The same point is made elsewhere with a different image, when the Quran says that \"when on the Day of Judgment the ˹whole˺ earth will be in His Grip, and the heavens will be rolled up in His Right Hand.\" The mountain question is really a question about whether God can end and renew His creation, and the answer begins with the mountains themselves dissolving. The verse also contains a comfort hidden inside the destruction. If God can wipe the mountains away, then He can also rebuild what He pleases, and the resurrection the Prophet kept announcing is no heavier for God than the mountains' removal. The same power that levels the old world will raise the new one, and the questioners were being shown the size of the hand that holds both. The next verses describe the ground that will be left after they are gone.",
  "106": "\"leaving the earth level and bare\" continues the description of what happens after the mountains are wiped out, and the image is of a clean sweep. The word for \"level\" renders 'qā'an,' a flat open plain, and \"bare\" renders 'safsafan,' a surface with nothing on it, smooth and empty. The earth that was crowded with mountains, valleys, cities, and ruins will be one level field, with nothing standing and nothing hiding. The verse is short, but the picture it builds is complete: a world stripped to its floor, ready for a single gathering.\n\nThe commentators note the purpose behind the flattening. The Day of Judgment requires a place where all of humanity can stand together, with no walls, no cover, and no distance, and the earth is prepared for exactly that. The leveling is also a leveling of everyone on it. The palaces and the caves, the fortresses of the rich and the hiding places of the runaway, are all gone, and every person stands on the same bare ground, exposed in the same way. Nothing of the old world remains to give anyone advantage or shelter.\n\nThe verse is a sentence long, and its lesson is in its starkness. The whole architecture of worldly life, everything people built and climbed and hid behind, is scenery. When the Day comes, the scenery is removed, and what remains is the level ground and the people on it, each one alone with what they did. The flattening of the earth is also the Quran's most visual answer to the idea that anything of this world can be taken along. People spend their lives accumulating positions and properties, and the verse shows the Day arriving with a clean sweep: no wall, no vault, no elevation, nothing carried over but the deeds. The next verse completes the picture with one more stroke: not even the ground itself will have any shape to it.",
  "107": "\"with neither depressions nor elevations to be seen\" finishes the portrait of the leveled earth, and the wording is exact about what kind of ground it will be. The word for \"depressions\" means dips and hollows, the low places of the earth, and the word for \"elevations\" means rises and bumps. Neither will exist. The ground will be perfectly flat and perfectly smooth, a surface without even the smallest fold in it. The commentators explain the choice of words: the verse is excluding both the low and the high, so that nothing on the earth remains that could shelter one person from another, or from the view of the gathering.\n\nThe detail matters because of what it removes. In this world, the shape of the land is the shape of human advantage. Armies hold the high ground; fugitives use the valleys; wealth builds on hills and poverty hides in hollows. On the Day of Judgment, the earth's shape itself is erased, and with it every positional advantage any human being ever had. Everyone is visible, everyone is equal, and everyone is exposed. The same leveling runs through the whole scene: the mountains are gone, the ground is bare, and the faces will be raised toward the caller with nowhere else to look.\n\nThe verse closes the answer to the question about the mountains, and the answer has moved from destruction to preparation. The mountains are not merely destroyed; the ground is made ready for the single greatest gathering of all time. The earth becomes a plain, and the plain becomes the court of the final judgement, with every human being standing on it. The three verses about the mountains and the earth, taken together, also teach a way of looking at the world. Mountains are not backdrops; they are loans. The solid ground is a stage that will be struck after the final act, and the one who forgets that the stage is temporary has misunderstood the play. The level plain is where the audience becomes the performers.",
  "108": "\"On that Day all will follow the caller ˹for assembly˺\" describes the beginning of the gathering, and the word \"caller\" is 'ad-da'i,' the one who calls and summons. The commentators identify this caller as an angel, the herald of the resurrection whose call assembles humanity, and the verse says that all will follow the call. No one will be late, no one will refuse, and no one will get lost, because the call will not merely be heard; it will be obeyed. The next phrase explains the obedience: \"˹and˺ none will dare to deviate.\" The Arabic behind \"deviate\" is the word for crookedness, the same word used for the uneven ground of the previous verses. Just as the earth will have no curves, the people will have no way to curve aside from the summons. Straight ground and straight movement: the whole scene is arranged so that every human being arrives.\n\nThen the verse turns to the sound of the place: \"All voices will be hushed before the Most Compassionate. Only whispers will be heard.\" The word \"hushed\" is 'khasha'at,' the lowering of voices in awe, and the result is a silence that no earthly crowd has ever produced. The billions gathered speak in whispers, and the whisper is described with the faintest word available. The verse chooses the name \"the Most Compassionate\" at this moment, and the choice is deliberate: the One before whom the universe falls silent is not only the Judge but the Merciful, and even His mercy is enough to hush every voice. The scene is the opposite of this world's noise, and the silence is the sound of everything finally recognizing who it stands before. The hushing of the voices is one of the Quran's most repeated scenes, and it always carries the same message: the Day of Judgment will not be a debate. The arguments that filled the world, the denials, the mockery, all of it will be over. What remains is the call, the obedience, and the silence, and inside the silence, judgement begins.",
  "109": "\"On that Day no intercession will be of any benefit\" announces the rule of the Day of Judgment, and it is a rule the Quran states many times because so much hangs on it. The word for \"intercession\" is 'ash-shafa'ah,' the pleading of one person on behalf of another, and in the world people are saved every day by connections: the friend at court, the relative with influence, the patron who speaks a word. The verse says that on that Day, all of it is dead. No connection, no rank, and no reputation will move the Judge by one step. Then comes the exception, and the exception is everything: \"except by those granted permission by the Most Compassionate and whose words are agreeable to Him.\" Intercession exists, but only under two conditions: God must permit the intercessor to speak, and the intercessor's words must be ones God approves. Permission and approval, both from Him.\n\nThe verse balances two truths that people often collapse into one. It shuts the door on the idea that anyone can be saved by someone else's influence, and it opens the door on the hope that God, in His mercy, will allow some to plead for others, the way the Prophet will plead for his community. Classical commentators explain that the intercession of that Day belongs to God's arrangement from beginning to end. No one owns it, and no one buys it. The verse leaves the believer in the right posture for that Day: not counting on any human connection, but hoping in the One who grants permission, and whose approval is the only currency the court accepts. The verse also guards against two opposite errors that have grown up around intercession. Some have hoped in it so much that they stopped working, as if a connection could replace a record. Others have denied it so thoroughly that they lost hope in God's mercy. The verse corrects both at once: intercession is real, but it belongs to God, and the heart should rest neither on human connections nor on despair, but on the One who permits and approves.",
  "110": "\"He ˹fully˺ knows what is ahead of them and what is behind them\" follows the verse on intercession with a statement of God's knowledge, and the two belong together. The Day of Judgment is possible, and its justice is perfect, because nothing about any human being is hidden from the Judge. What is ahead of them and what is behind them covers everything: their past and their future, their open deeds and their secrets, what they did and what they intended, what came before them and what will come after. The word \"fully\" closes every gap. There is no corner of any life that falls outside this knowledge.\n\nThe second half of the verse turns the direction around: \"but they cannot encompass Him in ˹their˺ knowledge.\" The verb \"encompass\" is 'yuhituna,' to surround and contain, and the verse says plainly that God's knowledge contains everything, while nothing contains God. Human knowledge, even at its largest, is knowledge from inside a small corner of creation. It cannot climb up and wrap itself around its Maker. The verse is both an assurance and a boundary: an assurance that the judgement will be built on complete knowledge, and a boundary that keeps the human mind humble about how much it can know of God Himself.\n\nThe verse also answers a worry that naturally follows from the previous one. If intercession depends on God's permission, does God actually know each person's case? The answer is this verse: He knows what is ahead and behind, so no case is mislaid, and no plea is heard without the full file. The court of that Day runs on perfect knowledge, and the verse states the perfection in both directions at once. There is also a practical fruit to this verse for the believer in this world. If God knows what is ahead and what is behind, then nothing that happens is a surprise to Him, and nothing that is done for Him is lost to Him. The knowledge that surrounds the Day of Judgment is the same knowledge that surrounds today, and it is the believer's quietest source of security.",
  "111": "\"And all faces will be humbled before the Ever-Living, All-Sustaining\" is the verse of the great standing, and every word in it is chosen for weight. The word \"humbled\" is 'anat,' and it describes faces lowered and necks bent, the body's admission of a power it cannot meet. In the world, people raise their faces to one another: to kings, to crowds, to their own pride. On that Day every face is lowered before God alone. The two names that follow are the reason for the lowering: \"the Ever-Living, All-Sustaining.\" These are 'al-Hayy' and 'al-Qayyum,' two of God's greatest names. The Ever-Living is the One who never dies, while every king and every tyrant dies; the All-Sustaining is the One who upholds everything, while every creature depends on being upheld. The verse places the whole of creation, living and dependent, before the One who lives by Himself and sustains all.\n\nThen the verdict: \"And those burdened with wrongdoing will be in loss.\" The word behind \"loss\" is 'khaba,' to fail and be disappointed, and it is the same word this chapter used when Moses warned the magicians that \"Whoever fabricates ˹lies˺ is bound to fail.\" The chapter is echoing itself. The fabricators failed in the arena; the wrongdoers will fail on the Day. The word \"burdened\" keeps the image of weight from the earlier verses alive: the wrongdoer does not walk into judgement empty-handed but loaded, and the load is his own injustice.\n\nThe verse is a complete courtroom in three lines: the posture of the accused, the identity of the Judge, and the sentence on the guilty. The name All-Sustaining also answers an unspoken question in the scene. The gathered billions are standing, and standing requires being held up, and the verse reminds them, in the middle of their terror, that the One before whom they stand is the One who has been holding them all along. The next verse turns to the other side of the courtroom, and the contrast is as sharp as the Quran can make it.",
  "112": "\"But whoever does good and is a believer will have no fear of being wronged or denied ˹their reward˺\" is the promise for the other half of the gathering, and its first word is the hinge. The previous verse lowered the faces of the wrongdoers and pronounced their loss; \"but\" turns the scene toward those who arrive with something else in their hands. The verse names what they carry: good deeds and belief. The two are paired the way they are paired throughout the Quran, because deeds without faith lack their foundation and faith without deeds lacks its fruit. The person of the promise is the one who has both.\n\nThe promise itself is stated through two fears and the removal of both. \"have no fear of being wronged\" addresses the fear of injustice, that a deed might be forgotten or its reward taken away. \"or denied ˹their reward˺\" addresses the fear of reduction, that a great deed might be counted small. The word behind \"denied\" means to be diminished and cut down, and the verse assures the believer that neither will happen. Nothing will be lost, and nothing will be shaved off. The reward will arrive whole. The two fears the verse removes are also the two fears of every worker, in every age. Will my work be remembered, and will it be paid in full? The verse answers both with the same word: no. Nothing will be forgotten, and nothing will be reduced.\n\nThe verse is a statement about the justice of God in its most comforting form. Human courts make mistakes, and human memories fade, and even the best employers underpay, but the accounting of that Day runs on the knowledge described in the surrounding verses. The believer's deeds are known, counted, and preserved, and the verse promises the full measure. After the silence and the terror of the scene, this verse is the first warmth in the chapter's description of the Day, and it lands exactly where the believer needs it.",
  "113": "\"And so We have sent it down as an Arabic Quran\" turns the chapter from the Day of Judgment back to the Book in the reader's hands, and the turn is deliberate. Everything just described, the trumpet, the level earth, the hushed crowd, is part of what this Book was sent to announce. The word \"Arabic\" matters because the Quran came in the language of the people it first addressed, clear to them without translation and without a priestly class standing between them and the words. God's final Book was not sent in a code for scholars. It was sent in a living language, so that ordinary people could hear it and understand what was being asked of them.\n\nThe next phrase explains how the Book does its work: \"and varied the warnings in it\". The verb 'sarrafa' means to turn and repeat something in different ways, and the warnings of the Quran are exactly that: the same truths about God, judgement, and resurrection, turned and presented from different angles, through different stories and different arguments, so that every kind of heart has a door it can enter. Then the purpose is stated: \"so perhaps they will shun evil or it may cause them to be mindful.\" The Arabic behind \"shun evil\" is 'taqwa,' the guarding awareness of God that keeps a person away from what God forbids, and the word behind \"mindful\" is 'dhikr,' remembrance. The Book aims at one of two results, and either one saves: the fear that restrains, or the remembrance that revives.\n\nThe verse is the Quran's own description of its method and its goal, and it invites the reader to test whether the method is working. The verse also implies a standard for anyone who speaks about religion. The Quran repeated its warnings in many forms so that hearts of every kind could be reached, and those who carry its message after it should learn the same patience with their own listeners.",
  "114": "\"Exalted is Allah, the True King!\" opens the verse with praise, and the praise is placed here because of what follows. The verse is about to give the Prophet instructions, and it begins by establishing who is giving them: God, exalted above all, the King whose kingship is real. Every other king borrows his crown and returns it; God's kingship is His own and never ends. The instruction that follows is one of the most personal in the Quran: \"Do not rush to recite ˹a revelation of˺ the Quran ˹O Prophet˺ before it is ˹properly˺ conveyed to you\". The background is well known from the reports of the Prophet's life. When Gabriel brought the revelation, the Prophet would move his tongue quickly, repeating the words as they arrived, afraid that he might lose them, and God taught him to wait, to let the revelation settle, and to trust that its preservation was not his burden. The same instruction appears elsewhere in the words \"Do not rush your tongue trying to memorize ˹a revelation of˺ the Quran.\" The Messenger was being taught, in front of all his followers, that even the Prophet receives patiently.\n\nThen comes the supplication that has become famous: \"and pray\", and the prayer itself is recorded: \"My Lord! Increase me in knowledge\". The Arabic is 'zidni ilma,' increase me in knowledge, and it is the only thing God ever commanded the Prophet to ask for more of. Not more wealth, not more years, but more knowledge. The verse draws the whole picture of how revelation is received: praise God, be patient with His words, and keep asking Him for more understanding. The request has belonged to every student and every believer since, and it never stops being the right prayer for the person holding this Book. And the knowledge requested is not only information; it is understanding that draws the heart closer to its Lord, the kind that turns every verse into a door.",
  "115": "“And indeed, We once made a covenant with Adam, but he forgot, and ˹so˺ We did not find determination in him.”” records the covenant, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the covenant: “And indeed, We once made a covenant with Adam, but he forgot, and so We did not find determination in him,” or “wa laqad ‘ahidna ila Adama min qablu fa-nasiya wa lam najid lahu ‘azman,” and the commentators note the covenant, for the Lord made the covenant with Adam, and the verse is the record of the covenant, the introduction of the story that the following verses unfold, and the commentators note the significance, for the covenant is the command, and the forgetting is the slip, and the verse is the record of the covenant, the introduction of the story of the fall. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the covenant is the command, and the forgetting is the slip, and the verse is the record of the covenant, the introduction that the following verses unfold.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the covenant, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own constancy. The believer is taught that the covenant of the Lord is the trust, and the one who forgets is the one whose forgetfulness the Lord weighs, and the believer who holds the covenant is the believer whose constancy the Lord values, and the covenant of Adam is the teaching, the declaration that the command is the trust, and the believer who reads the covenant is the believer whose constancy the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weight of the covenant: Adam forgot and repented, and the believer who holds the covenant is the believer whose constancy the Lord values, and the covenant is the teaching that the command is the trust. The verse is the record of the covenant, and the teaching of the constancy that the trust requires.",
  "116": "“And ˹remember˺ when We said to the angels, “Prostrate before Adam,” so they all did—but not Iblîs, who refused ˹arrogantly˺.”” records the prostration, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the prostration: “when We said to the angels, ‘Prostrate before Adam,’ so they all did—but not Iblis, who refused arrogantly,” or “wa ith qulna lil-mala’ikati usjudoo li-Adama fa-sajadoo illa Ibleesa aba,” and the commentators note the prostration, for the angels prostrated and Iblis refused, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the obedience and the refusal, and the commentators note the significance, for the prostration is the honour, and the refusal is the pride, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the test that the following verse warns of. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the prostration is the honour, and the refusal is the pride, and the verse is the record of the prostration, the test that the following verse warns of.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prostration, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own obedience. The believer is taught that the prostration is the obedience, and the one who obeys is the one whose obedience the Lord values, and the believer who bows to the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the prostration of the angels is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the duty, and the believer who reads the prostration is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the obedience of the prostration: the angels bowed and Iblis refused, and the believer who obeys is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the prostration is the teaching that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the prostration, and the teaching of the obedience that the command requires.",
  "117": "“So We cautioned, “O Adam! This is surely an enemy to you and to your wife. So do not let him drive you both out of Paradise, for you ˹O Adam˺ would then suffer ˹hardship˺.”” records the warning, and the commentators note the two parts of the warning. The verse records the warning: “O Adam! This is surely an enemy to you and to your wife. So do not let him drive you both out of Paradise, for you O Adam would then suffer hardship,” or “fa-qulna ya Adamu inna hatha ‘aduwwun laka wa li-zawjika fa-la yukhrijannakuma mina al-jannati fa-tashqa,” and the commentators note the warning, for the Lord warned Adam of the enemy, and the verse is the record of the warning, the caution that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the warning is the mercy, and the enemy is the danger, and the verse is the record of the warning, the caution of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the warning is the mercy, and the enemy is the danger, and the verse is the record of the warning, the caution that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the warning, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own vigilance. The believer is taught that the enemy is the danger, and the one who is warned is the one whose vigilance the Lord values, and the believer who guards against the enemy is the believer whose vigilance the Lord accepts, and the warning to Adam is the teaching, the declaration that the enemy is the thing that the believer must guard against, and the believer who reads the warning is the believer whose vigilance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the warning of the enemy: Adam was warned and fell, and the believer who guards against the enemy is the believer whose vigilance the Lord values, and the warning is the teaching that the enemy is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the warning, and the teaching of the vigilance that the enemy requires.",
  "118": "“Here it is guaranteed that you will never go hungry or unclothed,” records the first guarantee, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the guarantee: “Here it is guaranteed that you will never go hungry or unclothed,” or “inna laka alla tajoo‘a feeha wa la ta‘ra,” and the commentators note the guarantee, for the Lord guaranteed the provision, and the verse is the record of the guarantee, the first of the two, and the commentators note the significance, for the guarantee is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the guarantee, the first of the two mercies. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the guarantee is the mercy, and the provision is the gift, and the verse is the record of the guarantee, the first of the two.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the guarantee, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the provision of the Lord is the gift, and the one who is provided is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the provision is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the guarantee to Adam is the teaching, the declaration that the provision is the mercy, and the believer who reads the guarantee is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gratitude of the provision: Adam was guaranteed and was ungrateful, and the believer who thanks the provision is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the guarantee is the teaching that the provision is the gift. The verse is the record of the guarantee, and the teaching of the gratitude that the provision requires.",
  "119": "“nor will you ˹ever˺ suffer from thirst or ˹the sun’s˺ heat.”” completes the guarantee, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the guarantee: “nor will you ever suffer from thirst or the sun’s heat,” or “wa annaka la tazma’u feeha wa la tadha,” and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord completed the guarantee, and the verse is the record of the completion, the second of the two mercies, and the commentators note the significance, for the four provisions are the food and the clothing and the drink and the shade, and the verse is the record of the completion, the second of the two. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the mercy, and the provisions are the gifts, and the verse is the record of the completion, the second of the two mercies.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the provision of the Lord is the complete, and the one who is provided is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the gifts is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the completion of the guarantee is the teaching, the declaration that the provision is the full, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the fullness of the provision: the four were granted, and the believer who thanks the gifts is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the provision is the full. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the gratitude that the gifts require.",
  "120": "“But Satan whispered to him, saying, “O Adam! Shall I show you the Tree of Immortality and a kingdom that does not fade away?”” records the whisper, and the commentators note the two parts of the whisper. The verse records the whisper: “But Satan whispered to him, saying, ‘O Adam! Shall I show you the Tree of Immortality and a kingdom that does not fade away?’” or “fa-waswasa ilayhi ash-shaytanu qala ya Adamu hal adulluka ‘ala shajarati al-khuldi wa mulkin la yabla,” and the commentators note the whisper, for Satan whispered the promise, and the whisper is the deception, the offering of the false, and the verse is the record of the whisper, the deception that the following verse shows the effect of, and the commentators note the significance, for the promise of the immortality and the kingdom is the lie, and the verse is the record of the whisper, the deception of the enemy. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the whisper is the deception, and the promise is the lie, and the verse is the record of the whisper, the deception that the following verse shows the effect of.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the whisper, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own vigilance. The believer is taught that the whisper of the enemy is the deception, and the one who listens is the one whose fall the Lord records, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose vigilance the Lord values, and the whisper to Adam is the teaching, the declaration that the promise of the enemy is the lie, and the believer who reads the whisper is the believer whose vigilance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the deception of the enemy: the promise was the lie, and the believer who guards the heart is the believer whose vigilance the Lord values, and the whisper is the teaching that the promise of the enemy is the false. The verse is the record of the whisper, and the teaching of the vigilance that the enemy requires.",
  "121": "“So they both ate from the tree and then their nakedness was exposed to them, prompting them to cover themselves with leaves from Paradise. So Adam disobeyed his Lord, and ˹so˺ lost his way.”” records the fall, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the fall: “So they both ate from the tree and then their nakedness was exposed to them… So Adam disobeyed his Lord, and so lost his way,” or “fa-akala minha fa-badat lahuma saw’atuhuma wa tafiqa yakhsifani ‘alayhima min waraqi al-jannati wa ‘asa Adamu rabbahu fa-ghawa,” and the commentators note the fall, for the two ate and the nakedness was exposed, and the verse is the record of the fall, the disobedience that the following verse answers with the mercy, and the commentators note the significance, for the fall is the slip, and the verse is the record of the fall, the disobedience of Adam. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the fall is the slip, and the disobedience is the error, and the verse is the record of the fall, the disobedience that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the fall, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own repentance. The believer is taught that the fall is the human, and the one who slips is the one whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the believer who returns after the slip is the believer whose return the Lord values, and the fall of Adam is the teaching, the declaration that the slip is the thing that the repentance repairs, and the believer who reads the fall is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the humanity of the slip: Adam fell and repented, and the believer who returns after the slip is the believer whose return the Lord values, and the fall is the teaching that the slip is the thing that the mercy repairs. The verse is the record of the fall, and the teaching of the hope that the repentance carries.",
  "122": "“Then his Lord chose him ˹for His grace˺, accepted his repentance, and guided him ˹rightly˺.”” records the mercy, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the mercy: “Then his Lord chose him for His grace, accepted his repentance, and guided him rightly,” or “thumma ijtabahu rabbuhu fa-taba ‘alayhi wa hada,” and the commentators note the mercy, for the Lord chose Adam and accepted the repentance, and the verse is the record of the mercy, the acceptance that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the three acts are the choosing and the acceptance and the guidance, and the verse is the record of the mercy, the acceptance of the repentance. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the choosing is the election, and the acceptance is the mercy, and the guidance is the gift, and the verse is the record of the mercy, the acceptance that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mercy, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the repentance is the thing that the Lord accepts, and the one who returns is the one whom the Lord chooses, and the believer who repents is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the mercy to Adam is the teaching, the declaration that the return is the path of the acceptance, and the believer who reads the mercy is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the acceptance of the repentance: Adam was chosen and accepted, and the believer who returns is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the mercy is the teaching that the return is the thing that the Lord receives. The verse is the record of the mercy, and the teaching of the hope that the acceptance carries.",
  "123": "“Allah said, “Descend, both of you, from here together ˹with Satan˺ as enemies to each other. Then when guidance comes to you from Me, whoever follows My guidance will neither go astray ˹in this life˺ nor suffer ˹in the next˺.”” records the descent, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the descent: “Descend, both of you, from here together with Satan as enemies to each other. Then when guidance comes to you from Me, whoever follows My guidance will neither go astray in this life nor suffer in the next,” or “qala ihbita minha jamee‘an ba‘dukum li-ba‘din ‘aduwwun fa-imma ya’tiyannakum minnee hudan famani ittaba‘a hudaya fa-la yadillu wa la yashqa,” and the commentators note the descent, for the Lord commanded the descent and promised the guidance, and the verse is the record of the descent, the promise that the following verses develop, and the commentators note the significance, for the descent is the test, and the guidance is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the descent, the promise of the guidance. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the descent is the test, and the guidance is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the descent, the promise that the following verses develop.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the descent, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the guidance of the Lord is the safety, and the one who follows the guidance is the one whom the Lord protects, and the believer who holds the guidance is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the promise to Adam is the teaching, the declaration that the guidance is the path of the safety, and the believer who reads the descent is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the promise of the guidance: the descent and the guidance, and the believer who follows the guidance is the believer whose safety the Lord grants, and the promise is the teaching that the guidance is the thing that the believer must follow. The verse is the record of the descent, and the teaching of the hope that the guidance carries.",
  "124": "\"But whoever turns away from My Reminder\" is the other half of the promise, and the word \"but\" sets it against everything the previous verse guaranteed. The follower of guidance is promised safety; the one who turns away from the Reminder is given a different future, and it begins in this world: \"will certainly have a miserable life\". The Arabic phrase is 'ma'ishatan danka,' a life that is narrow and constricted, and the commentators explain that the constriction is not necessarily poverty. A person may be wealthy and still live the most constricted of lives, because the narrowness is in the chest, not the wallet. The heart that turns away from its Lord has no room to breathe, whatever it owns. The word \"certainly\" makes the misery a law rather than a possibility: turning away from the Reminder and peace of heart cannot coexist.\n\nThe second half of the verse moves to the next world: \"then We will raise them up blind on the Day of Judgment.\" The person who had eyes and refused to see will be raised without sight, and the blindness is the outer form of the inner choice. The Quran states the principle elsewhere: \"But whoever is blind ˹to the truth˺ in this ˹world˺ will be blind in the Hereafter, and ˹even˺ far more astray from the ˹Right˺ Way.\" The verse is not a threat alone. It is a description of a cause and its effect, drawn in two worlds. The constriction and the blindness are what a person chooses when they choose to turn away, and the next verse records the cry of the one who discovers the choice too late. The two halves of the verse also belong together. The constricted life is the seed, and the blindness is the harvest. A person does not become blind on the Day of Judgment by surprise; the blindness is grown, day by day, out of every reminder that was turned away from.",
  "125": "\"My Lord! Why have you raised me up blind, although I used to see?\" is the cry of the person described in the previous verse, and it is placed in the reader's ears before the answer is given. The raised person feels the injustice of their state and protests it: I could see, so why am I blind? The question is real, and the Quran does not mock it. It lets the sufferer state their case in full, because the answer that follows will be the answer to every complaint ever made against God's justice. The blindness they protest is the very thing their life was building, and the next verse will show them the connection they never made while they could see.\n\nThe verse also carries a subtle reversal. In the world, the person who turns away from revelation walks past the truth daily, using their sight for everything except the one thing it was for. They see food and drink and profit, and they do not see the signs. On the Day of Judgment, the arrangement is reversed: the outer sight is gone, and the only thing that would have helped was the inner sight they never developed. The Quran elsewhere describes the state in full: the one who was blind to the truth here will be blind there and more astray from the way. The cry in this verse is the sound of that discovery, and it is recorded so that the reader makes the discovery now, while the sight is still in their possession and the choice is still open. The question is also the last echo of the excuses people make in this world. In life, they say they did not know, or did not see, or were not told. On that Day, the cry changes shape but keeps the same claim, and the answer that follows shows that every excuse was already anticipated and already answered.",
  "126": "\"It is so, just as Our revelations came to you and you neglected them, so Today you are neglected\" is God's answer to the cry, and it is the Quran's clearest statement of the principle that the Hereafter gives back what this world sent forward. The word \"neglected\" renders 'nasita,' which means to forget and to leave behind. The person did not merely reject the revelations; they neglected them, treated them as nothing, let them slide out of their life, and the answer says: today, you are treated the same way. The measure is exact. What they did to God's signs is what is now done to them, not out of spite but out of the perfect justice that returns every deed in its own shape.\n\nThe verse also answers a question that the wording might raise. Does this mean God forgets? The same chapter has already closed that door, in Moses's words about his Lord: \"My Lord neither falters nor forgets ˹anything˺.\" God's forgetting here is not the loss of memory. It is the leaving and the disregarding, the abandonment that mirrors their own, the way a person is said to have forgotten something they deliberately stopped caring about. The punishment is made of the sin itself. They neglected, and they are neglected; they turned away, and they are left. The verse is frightening precisely because it is fair, and its fairness is what the reader is meant to feel: nothing in that answer could be protested, because it is only the echo of their own life, returned to them in full. The verse is also a warning about the form neglect takes. Most people who turn away from the truth do not fight it; they simply let it slide, postponing it, until it is no longer part of their lives. The punishment mirrors that quietness. The neglect that felt so light in the world returns on the Day with its full weight.",
  "127": "\"This is how We reward whoever transgresses and does not believe in the revelations of their Lord\" turns the single scene into a general law. The person who was raised blind was not an exception; the verse says plainly that this is the pattern, the standard recompense for everyone who lives the same way. Two faults are named together: transgression, which is crossing the limits of God, and disbelief in His revelations, which is rejecting the guidance He sent. The two usually travel together, because the person who does not believe has no reason to respect the limits, and the person who tramples the limits has usually already stopped believing in the One who set them.\n\nThen the verse adds the sentence that has echoed through this entire chapter: \"And the punishment of the Hereafter is far more severe and more lasting.\" The Arabic words for \"more severe\" and \"more lasting\" are 'ashaddu wa abqa,' and they are the very words the believing magicians used when they answered Pharaoh's threat, declaring that \"Allah is far superior ˹in reward˺ and more lasting ˹in punishment˺.\" The chapter is weaving its own argument together. The magicians said it in the heat of their stand, and now God confirms it as His own verdict. Whatever suffering the wrongdoer meets in this world is a sample. The full weight is in the Hereafter, and the permanence there is the point: worldly punishment ends, but the punishment of the Hereafter is \"more lasting\" than anything the world can contain. The verse is the chapter's way of keeping the two worlds on the scale together, so that no one ever mistakes the sample for the whole. The verse also teaches the reader to keep the two punishments in their proper order. People fear the penalties of this world, which are visible and near. The Quran keeps repeating that the unseen penalty is the real one, and the phrase \"more lasting\" is aimed at exactly that misjudgement: the heavier punishment is the one the world cannot show you.",
  "128": "\"Is it not yet clear to them how many peoples We destroyed before them, whose ruins they still pass by?\" is a question built on geography. The people of Mecca, to whom these verses were first addressed, traveled north and south for trade every year, and their routes passed through the ruins of nations the Quran names elsewhere: the homes of the people of Noah, the carved dwellings of Thamud, the cities of the people of Lot, all of them destroyed for the same rejection that Mecca was repeating. The verse asks: has the road not guided them yet? The word for \"clear\" comes from the root of guidance, so the question is literally: has the truth not yet led them, when the evidence is standing on their own trade routes?\n\nThe ruins are described as something they \"still pass by\", and the passing is the point. The destroyed nations are not distant legends. They are landmarks on the daily road, visible walls and empty dwellings, and the travelers pass them without a thought. The verse then closes with the phrase that has appeared before in this chapter: \"Surely in this are signs for people of sound judgment.\" The ruins are signs, but signs only work for the mind that stops and thinks, the mind the earlier verses called 'uli an-nuha,' the intellect that restrains its owner. The verse is the Quran's version of archaeology: history is not entertainment, and ruins are not scenery. Every empty city on the road is a message, and the message is always the same: this is what happened to the people who lived the way you are living. The question is whether the traveler will read it before the road runs out. The question is also aimed at the reader in every age, because every land has its ruins and every people has its roads. The question never changes; only the travelers change, and the ruins wait for each new generation to pass by and answer it.",
  "129": "\"Had it not been for a prior decree from your Lord ˹O Prophet˺ and a term already set\" explains why the punishment described in the surrounding verses does not fall on the mockers immediately, and the explanation is the secret of the world's patience. The word for \"decree\" is 'kalimah,' a word, and the commentators explain it as the word of God that went before, the decision that the punishment of these nations is deferred to the Hereafter, and the \"term already set\" is the appointed hour of every person and every people, fixed and known to God. The verse then states the consequence: \"their ˹instant˺ doom would have been inevitable.\" The word for \"inevitable\" is 'lazim,' something stuck fast, and the picture is of a punishment that would have attached itself to them at once, without delay.\n\nThe verse answers an old question: why does the wrongdoer seem to go unpunished? The answer is not that God does not see, and it is not that the deeds do not matter. The answer is that a prior decree and a set term hold the punishment back, and the holding back is mercy as much as justice. It is mercy, because the delay gives every person time to turn, and the same delay that lets a tyrant breathe lets a sinner repent. It is justice, because when the term arrives, the punishment comes in full and nothing is lost. The verse also comforts the Prophet and the believers, who watched the mockers prosper and wondered how long the arrangement could last. The arrangement, the verse says, has an expiry date written on it, and the mockers are not escaping; they are waiting. The verse is also a comfort for anyone who has ever felt that the world's accounts never seem to balance. The balance exists, and the ledger is open, but the settlement is scheduled, and the verse says the schedule was set before the world began. The next verse will tell the Prophet what to do while the wait lasts.",
  "130": "\"So be patient ˹O Prophet˺ with what they say\" turns the chapter toward its practical conclusion, and the patience commanded here is not passive endurance. It is the patience of the messenger who keeps his task while the mockery continues, and the verse immediately supplies the fuel for that patience: \"And glorify the praises of your Lord before sunrise and before sunset, and glorify Him in the hours of the night and at both ends of the day.\" The word for glorify is 'sabbih,' to declare God far above everything unworthy of Him, and the verse spreads this glorification across the whole day. The commentators explain that these times cover the daily prayers: before sunrise, before sunset, the hours of the night, and both ends of the day, the five points at which the believer turns to God and the day is anchored. The same instruction appears elsewhere in nearly identical words: \"So be patient ˹O Prophet˺ with what they say. And glorify the praises of your Lord before sunrise and before sunset.\"\n\nThe connection between the patience and the glorification is the heart of the verse. A person cannot hold steady against mockery on their own reserves. The reserves are renewed in prayer, at fixed times, every day, and the one whose day is anchored in glorification can bear what the day brings. The verse ends with the reward: \"so that you may be pleased ˹with the reward˺.\" The Arabic 'tarda' means to be pleased and satisfied, and the commentators say the promise is the pleasure of God's reward, the contentment that comes to the one who persisted. Patience, prayer, and the pleasure at the end: the verse hands the Prophet, and every believer, the complete equipment for the long road. The verse also shows the shape of a believer's day: begun in glorification, continued in patience, and closed in the hope of God's reward. The mockers have their noise, and the believer has these fixed points, and the fixed points hold.",
  "131": "\"Do not let your eyes crave what We have allowed some of the disbelievers to enjoy\" is the instruction that follows the command to be patient, and it is the inner side of the same discipline. The Arabic idiom is vivid: do not stretch your eyes toward something, do not keep them fixed on it, and the something is the enjoyment of the disbelievers. Patience against mockery is hard, but the harder test is watching the mockers prosper while the believers struggle, and the verse addresses that test directly. The Prophet is told not to let his eyes dwell on what others have been given, because the looking itself is the first step of the wanting. The same instruction appears elsewhere with a matching addition: \"Do not let your eyes crave the ˹fleeting˺ pleasures We have provided for some of the disbelievers, nor grieve for them. And be gracious to the believers.\"\n\nThe verse then explains what the enjoyed things actually are: \"the ˹fleeting˺ splendour of this worldly life, which We test them with.\" Two words do the work. It is splendour, so the enjoyment is real and visible, and it is fleeting, so it ends. And it is a test, 'fitnah,' not a reward: the wealth and comfort of the disbelievers are the questions on their exam paper, not the prizes for their answers. Then the verse gives the comparison that resets the scale: \"But your Lord’s provision ˹in the Hereafter˺ is far better and more lasting.\" The words \"better and more lasting\" are the same pair the chapter has used before, the words of the magicians and of the verdict on the wrongdoers. What God keeps for His servants is better in kind and longer in duration than anything the eyes could crave, and the verse's cure for envy is exactly this: look at what is being compared, and look at how long each one lasts.",
  "132": "\"Bid your people to pray, and be diligent in ˹observing˺ it\" places the responsibility for prayer in the household, and the instruction has never been only for prophets. The Arabic word rendered \"your people\" is 'ahlaka,' your family, and the command is to call them to prayer and to keep them on it. The second phrase, \"be diligent in ˹observing˺ it\", uses the word 'wastabir,' which means to be constant and patient over something, to hold to it without letting go. Prayer is not a habit that runs itself; it is a discipline that must be commanded, guarded, and persisted in, and the verse gives the believer the standing order for both.\n\nThen comes the sentence that has carried so many worried hearts: \"We do not ask you to provide. It is We Who provide for you.\" The verse joins prayer and provision deliberately, because they are the two great worries of a household: will we worship as we should, and will we eat? God takes the second worry off the table. He does not ask the servant to earn the family's provision; He asks for the prayer, and He Himself is the Provider. The commentators note how this sentence has been the refuge of every believer who feared that devotion would cost their livelihood. Worship does not compete with provision, because the Provider commanded the worship.\n\nThe verse closes with the principle that arranges everything: \"And the ultimate outcome is ˹only˺ for ˹the people of˺ righteousness.\" The word for righteousness is 'taqwa,' the guarded awareness of God, and the good end belongs to those who have it. The family that prays and trusts is not losing anything. The end is theirs, and the verse says it as a law rather than a wish: the final outcome belongs to the people of taqwa, and everything before the end is just the road.",
  "133": "\"If only he could bring us a sign from his Lord!\" is the demand the disbelievers repeat, and it is the old request in its plainest form: show us something extraordinary, the way earlier prophets showed their people. The verse answers with a question that turns the demand back on them: \"Have they not ˹already˺ received a confirmation of what is in earlier Scriptures?\" The word behind \"confirmation\" is 'baiyyinah,' clear evidence, and the evidence named here is the knowledge contained in the earlier Scriptures, the Torah and the Gospel, which described the coming of this final Prophet. The people who had the Scriptures knew, or could know, that he was foretold, and the Quran itself, with its knowledge of the past and its truth about God, was the sign standing in front of them. The demand for a new sign was the demand of people who had closed their eyes to the sign they already held.\n\nThe same question appears elsewhere in the Quran, where the demanders are told: \"Allah certainly has the power to send down a sign\"—though most of them do not know.\" The point is not that God cannot produce signs. The point is that signs are not the obstacle. Pharaoh received signs and did not believe; the magicians saw one sign and believed. The difference was never the sign but the heart. The verse teaches the reader to recognize the demand for what it often is: a way of postponing the decision. Those who are waiting for a sign they will never accept are not waiting for evidence. They are waiting for permission to ignore the evidence they already have, and the verse removes the excuse before they can use it. The verse is also an invitation to look at the Quran itself as the sign. Its language, its knowledge of the past, and its consistency over the long years of revelation are all evidence that no human author could have produced it. The sign they demanded was in their hands, being recited to them.",
  "134": "\"Had We destroyed them with a torment before this ˹Prophet came˺, they would have surely argued\" opens a window into the justice of God's arrangement, and the window shows the excuse that the people themselves would have made. The verse imagines the alternative: if God had destroyed them without sending a messenger, they would have raised exactly this protest: \"Our Lord! If only You had sent us a messenger, we would have followed Your revelations before being humiliated and put to shame.\" The imagined plea is placed in their own mouths, and it is the strongest argument they could ever make against being punished without warning. The Quran grants them the argument, and then answers it: the messenger has come. The very condition they would have demanded has been fulfilled, and the excuse is dead before it is born.\n\nThe verse is part of a principle the Quran states again and again: God does not punish a people until He has sent them a messenger to warn them, in the words recorded elsewhere, \"And We would never punish ˹a people˺ until We have sent a messenger ˹to warn them˺.\" The sending of messengers is not an act of anger but an act of fairness, because judgement without warning would give every guilty person a defense. The verse also contains a warning inside its logic. The people who now have the messenger cannot later say they were not told. The proof is standing among them, and the imagined plea has been recorded in the Quran itself, so that on the Day it is raised, the answer will already be on record. The verse also explains why the messengers always came before the punishments in the stories this chapter has been telling. Every destroyed nation in the Quran was warned first, and the warning is recorded so that the destruction can never be called unjust. The messenger is the proof that the punishment was deserved.",
  "135": "\"Each ˹of us˺ is waiting, so keep waiting!\" is the final word of the chapter, and it is a word of calm finality. The Prophet is told to say it to the people who mocked and demanded signs: we are all waiting, you and I, so wait. The Arabic word is 'mutarabbis,' one who watches and waits for an outcome, and the verse gives the sentence a strange serenity. There is no threat in the wording, no anger, only the statement that both sides are now waiting for the same thing: the end of the story. The disbelievers are waiting for their triumph, and the Prophet is waiting for God's promise, and the verse says that the wait itself will be the verdict.\n\nThe second half states what the wait will reveal: \"You will soon know who is on the Straight Path and is ˹rightly˺ guided.\" The question the whole chapter has been circling, the question of Pharaoh and Moses, of the magicians and the calf, of the mockers and the Prophet, is handed to the future to answer. The word \"soon\" is the Quran's way of saying that the answer is nearer than the mockers think, and the answer will not come as an argument but as an outcome. The chapter that began with the letters Ta-Ha and the story of Moses's selection ends with this quiet ultimatum: wait, and you will see who was on the Straight Path. For the believer, the verse is a release. The debate is over, the duty is done, and the outcome belongs to God. For the mocker, it is the last warning in the book of warnings: the wait is not a postponement. It is the beginning of the judgement. The verse is also a model of how a believer ends a long argument: not with a shout but with a boundary. The Prophet has delivered, the mockers have heard, and the rest belongs to God and to time. The Straight Path is not decided by votes; it is revealed by outcomes."
};
